(window.MUSEUM_READ_T = window.MUSEUM_READ_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"lead":"How were games found, forgotten, decoded and changed? Read {n} stories; answer every question of the quiz at the end to earn a stamp.","card":"Stamp card","cardN":"{n} of {of} collected","cardAll":"All collected!","no":"Story {n}","mins":"{n} min","stamped":"Stamped","back":"All stories","quiz":"Quick quiz","links":"See and play","game":"Game","exhibit":"Collection","art":"Painting","room":"Room","room_chance":"Chance","qn":"Question {n} of {of}","right":"Correct!","wrong":"Not quite — the right answer is marked.","got":"Stamped!","again":"All correct!","gotSub":"It is on your stamp card.","missed":"{n} wrong","retrySub":"Get every question right to earn the stamp. Read the story again and retry.","retry":"Try again","room_paint":"In art","room_map":"Map","kfH":"Key points","tlH":"Timeline","refH":"Sources and further reading","picLink":"opens the museum’s page"},"ex":{"ur":"Royal Game of Ur board","liubo":"Liubo board and pieces","pyrgus":"Roman dice tower","sugoroku":"Shōsōin sugoroku board","nishapur":"Nishapur chess set","lewis":"Lewis chessmen","senet":"Tutankhamun’s senet box","ballinderry":"Ballinderry game board","goose":"Chess and goose game board","kofukuji":"Shogi pieces from Kōfuku-ji","shosoin":"Shōsōin go board"},"paint":{"liubo":"Two figures playing liubo","sugoroku":"Two girls playing sugoroku","shahnama":"Buzurgmihr masters chess","nefertari":"Queen Nefertari playing senet","chaupar":"Shiva and Parvati playing chaupar","shogi":"A young couple playing shogi","hydria":"Achilles and Ajax Playing a Board Game (water jar)","eakins":"The Chess Players","weiqi":"Dish with immortals playing weiqi","webb":"The Backgammon Players (cabinet)","liberale":"The Chess Players","mirror":"Mirror case with a couple playing chess","morris":"A gaming board (Nine Men’s Morris)","leifeng":"Leifeng Pagoda board game","teniers":"Game of Backgammon","imari":"Dish with two men playing go","shoei":"The Four Accomplishments","tutinama":"The monkey bites the prince, his chess partner (Mughal, c. 1560)","ingram":"The Game of Chinese Chess","umaya":"Horse Stable (go and shogi on the veranda)","kiyohiro":"Sugoroku Players","vuillard":"The Game of Checkers (from Landscapes and Interiors)","kangra":"Couple playing chaupar on a terrace","posada":"Game of the Goose","jintile":"Tile with Women Playing a Board Game","pvl":"Men Playing a Game of Dice"},"ur":{"title":"How a 4,500-year-old game got its rules back","lead":"The board from the royal tombs of Ur came without instructions. The clue was a clay tablet written two thousand years later.","s":["In the 1920s Leonard Woolley excavated the royal tombs at Ur in southern Iraq and found a board of twenty squares inlaid with shell and lapis lazuli, made around 2600–2400 BC. Similar boards have turned up across the Middle East, so the game was clearly played widely and for a long time.","But nothing explaining how to play came with them. A board, pieces and dice — and no rules: a familiar problem with ancient games.","The clue sat in the British Museum’s stores: a cuneiform tablet copied in 177/176 BC by the Babylonian scribe Itti-Marduk-balāṭu from a tablet belonging to a man named Iddin-Bēl. It was written more than two thousand years after the board was made.","In the early 1980s Irving Finkel of the British Museum realised the tablet described the same twenty-square game. Its back shows the board, with omens for landing on the squares. From the tablet and the board itself, Finkel reconstructed a race game for two players.","His reconstruction is now played all over the world. Whether the people of Ur played exactly like this, no one can say. Yet a similar game was still played in Kochi, in south India, into the 1950s — a sign of how long this game lived."],"quiz":[["When was the tablet that gave the rules written?","The 2nd century BC, over 2,000 years after the board","Around 2600 BC, like the board","The Middle Ages (12th century)","The 19th century"],["Who decoded the tablet and reconstructed the rules?","Irving Finkel","Howard Carter","Jean-François Champollion","Arthur Evans"],["What does the tablet say about landing on the squares?","Omens — good or bad fortune","The names of kings","Tax amounts","Only the names of stars"]],"kf":["The boards date from about 2600–2400 BC, from the royal tombs at Ur in southern Iraq","The rules were rebuilt from a clay tablet written more than 2,000 years later","20 squares, seven pieces a side, four-sided dice"],"more":[{"h":"Royal tombs and a “royal game”","p":["Woolley found not one twenty-square board in the Royal Cemetery at Ur but several. The best known, now in the British Museum, is wood inlaid with shell, red limestone and lapis lazuli, and it gave the game its name, “the Royal Game of Ur”.","Yet it was no game for kings alone. Boards of the same twenty squares turn up from Mesopotamia to Egypt and the eastern Mediterranean, and in an Assyrian palace a board was even scratched into the base of a great human-headed winged bull guarding a gate — apparently by the guards."],"cap":["The Royal Game of Ur in the British Museum — turn it round in 3D in the Collection room"]},{"h":"How the board works","p":["The twenty squares are a block of twelve (3×4) and a block of six (3×2) joined by a narrow “bridge” of two. Five squares bear a rosette; in the reconstructed rules, landing on one gives another throw and a square where you cannot be taken.","The dice are tetrahedra with two of their four points marked. You throw four and move by the number of marked points facing up, so the score runs from 0 to 4. Two is the commonest (6 times in 16); 0 and 4 come only once in 16. You can throw them yourself in the museum’s “Chance” room."],"cap":["The twenty squares and five rosettes. The green numbers are one player’s road, square by square (reconstructed rules)","How four tetrahedral dice fall (out of 16 equally likely ways)"]},{"h":"What the tablet says","p":["The front of the tablet explains the play; the back has a drawing of the board with signs of the zodiac in the squares. The sayings for landing on a square read like fortunes — “you will find a friend”, “you will drink fine beer” — so the game was tied to luck and omens.","Part of the tablet is broken, though, and not every rule can be read. Finkel’s reconstruction builds the most coherent game from the readable lines and the shape of the board; other scholars have proposed other versions."]},{"h":"The last players, in India","p":["Finkel later learned that the Jewish community of Kochi (Cochin) in southern India had played a twenty-square game until very recently, on a board of much the same shape.","It was handed down in their homes until they moved to Israel in the 1950s. The game may have travelled to India along ancient trade routes and stayed alive for more than four thousand years."]}],"tl":[["c. 2600–2400 BC","Twenty-square boards are laid in the royal tombs at Ur"],["8th century BC","A board is scratched on the base of a gate guardian in an Assyrian palace"],["177/176 BC","A Babylonian scribe copies the tablet with the rules"],["1922–1934","Woolley excavates Ur and finds the boards"],["Early 1980s","Finkel reads the tablet and reconstructs the rules"],["Until the 1950s","A very similar game is played in Kochi, southern India"]]},"liubo":{"title":"Why did liubo vanish?","lead":"Under the Han, everyone from the emperor down played it. Within a few centuries even its rules were forgotten.","s":["Liubo was the great craze of Han China (206 BC – AD 220). It appears in tomb figures, carved stone reliefs and even mirror designs, and immortals are often shown playing it.","After the Han fell, liubo declined quickly, and by the Tang dynasty its rules seem to have been lost. Why is not clear; one view is that players turned to other games such as weiqi (go).","What survived were boards and tools. The boards carry a distinctive pattern of T, L and V shapes, and moves were probably decided by throwing thin sticks. The same pattern decorates the so-called TLV mirrors.","Written clues are slowly gathering too. In 1993 a divination text using the board diagram was found in a Han tomb at Yinwan, Jiangsu. The tomb of Liu He, Marquis of Haihun, excavated in the 2010s, yielded more than a thousand bamboo slips about liubo, which are still being studied.","The Atlas’s liubo is a reconstruction built on such clues. Scholars reconstruct it in somewhat different ways, and the true rules are still not fully known — new finds may yet change how it is played."],"quiz":[["In which dynasty was liubo hugely popular?","Han","Ming","Qing","Song"],["Where were more than a thousand bamboo slips about liubo found?","The tomb of Liu He, Marquis of Haihun","The mausoleum of the First Emperor","The Dunhuang caves","The Forbidden City"],["What was probably thrown to decide moves in liubo?","Thin sticks","A twenty-sided die","A spinning top","Picture cards"]],"kf":["Hugely popular in China from the Warring States through the Han (5th century BC – 3rd century AD)","A board cut with lines shaped like T, L and V; thin sticks thrown for the move","It faded fast after the Han fell, and its rules are still not fully known"],"more":[{"h":"The TLV board","p":["A liubo board is square, with a square in the middle, a T at the middle of each side, L shapes around them and V shapes in the corners. The pieces are thought to have moved along and between these lines.","The same lines appear on the backs of mirrors (“TLV mirrors”) and on sundials. Some scholars read the board as a map of the cosmos — heaven, earth and the four directions — a hint that liubo was more than a pastime."],"cap":["The lines of a liubo board (the TLV pattern): a central square, a T on each side, L and V shapes toward the corners"]},{"h":"How it was played","p":["Most scholars think each player had six pieces and threw thin bamboo sticks to decide the move. The “six” (liu) in the name is said to come from those six sticks or six pieces.","A piece that advanced seems to have become a powerful “owl” (xiao). A poem of about the 3rd century BC, “Summoning the Soul” in the Songs of Chu, has the line “making an owl, they press on and call out ‘five whites!’”. Texts also describe a way of play in which pieces caught “fish” in the “water” at the centre of the board."],"cap":["Two figures playing liubo, made for a Han tomb (The Metropolitan Museum of Art)"]},{"h":"Sets from the tombs","p":["Tomb 3 at Mawangdui in Changsha, Hunan (about 168 BC), held a complete set — board, pieces and sticks — in a lacquered box, placed so the dead could play in the afterlife.","Relief stones from tomb walls often show immortals playing liubo on mountaintops. The game was tied to the world of the immortals and long life — a special kind of play."],"cap":["The museum’s liubo board — see it in 3D in the Collection room"]}],"tl":[["5th–3rd c. BC (Warring States)","Liubo boards begin to be placed in tombs"],["c. 3rd c. BC","“Summoning the Soul” in the Songs of Chu describes a game of liubo"],["c. 168 BC","A full set — board, pieces, sticks — is laid in Mawangdui tomb 3"],["206 BC – AD 220 (Han)","Played by emperors and commoners alike; on reliefs and mirrors"],["Tang (7th–10th c.)","The rules are thought to have been forgotten"],["1993","A divination text using the board appears from the Yinwan Han tombs"],["2010s","Bamboo slips on liubo are found in the tomb of the Marquis of Haihun, Liu He"]]},"zeno":{"title":"Emperor Zeno’s unlucky throw","lead":"Fifteen hundred years ago an emperor rolled the dice — and the result survives in a poem.","s":["Romans loved tabula, a race game on a board of 24 points played with dice — the ancestor of backgammon. To stop crooked throws they even used dice towers, letting the dice tumble down inside.","Around AD 480 the East Roman emperor Zeno was playing tabula. His three dice showed 2, 5 and 6. He had no choice but to move as the rules required, and his board was left with eight pieces standing alone and unprotected.","The game was recorded by Agathias, a 6th-century historian and poet who lived a few decades after Zeno. His short poem (an epigram) gives the position of the pieces, a rare record of an ancient board game in play.","In the 19th century the French scholar Becq de Fouquières worked out the position and the rules from the poem: fifteen pieces each and three dice. All pieces start off the board, and both players move in the same direction.","Compared with backgammon today, tabula used three dice, began with no pieces on the board, and had no doubling cube. Thanks to an emperor’s bad luck, we can see what tabula looked like."],"quiz":[["What did Zeno roll?","2, 5 and 6","6, 6 and 6","1, 1 and 1","3, 4 and 5"],["Who recorded the game in a poem?","Agathias","Homer","Pliny","Cicero"],["Which is a difference between tabula and backgammon today?","Tabula used three dice","Thirty pieces each","A board of 12 points","Pieces move backwards"]],"kf":["Tabula used 24 points and three dice — an ancestor of backgammon","Emperor Zeno’s game of about AD 480 survives, position and all, in a poem","The Romans even built “dice towers” to stop cheating"],"more":[{"h":"The tabula board","p":["A tabula board, like today’s backgammon board, has 24 points in two rows of 12. Each player had fifteen pieces, all starting off the board; following three dice, they were brought on, travelled round and were borne off.","The great difference from backgammon is that both players move in the same direction. A piece standing alone could be hit when the opponent landed on it, and had to come in again. Zeno’s eight were exactly such easily hit pieces."],"cap":["A tabula board: 24 points in two rows of 12; both players’ pieces travel the same way (arrow)"]},{"h":"A device against cheating","p":["Romans were mad about dice. Gambling was against the law, said to be allowed only during the winter festival of Saturnalia, but it never stopped, and so did sleight of hand with the dice.","Hence the dice tower: dice dropped in at the top bounce off boards inside and roll out at the bottom. A fourth-century tower from a villa in Germany is pierced with the words “The Picts are beaten, the enemy destroyed — play in safety.”"],"cap":["The dice tower of Vettweiß-Froitzheim (4th century) — see it in 3D in the Collection room"]},{"h":"Emperors and dice","p":["Zeno was not alone. The historian Suetonius says the first-century emperor Claudius loved dice so much that he wrote a book on the art and had his carriage fitted so the board would not slip while he played.","Roman sites also yield many boards of 36 letters set in six-letter words; each letter served as a square, and some carry teasing lines such as “Get up — you can’t play; make room for a player.”"],"cap":["Tables and dice were a pleasure from emperors down to soldiers (throw cubic dice in the Chance room)"]}],"tl":[["1st century","Emperor Claudius writes a book on dice-play"],["4th century","A dice tower is in use at a villa in Germany"],["c. 480","Emperor Zeno’s unlucky game"],["6th century","Agathias puts the game into verse"],["c. 10th century","The poem is gathered into the Greek Anthology"],["1869","Becq de Fouquières works out the position and the rules"],["1934","R. G. Austin re-examines Zeno’s game in detail"]]},"sugoroku":{"title":"The banned game of sugoroku","lead":"Emperors forbade it, people kept playing. The story of board sugoroku in Japan, from antiquity to the Edo period.","s":["The Shōsōin treasury keeps a sugoroku board said to have been a favourite of Emperor Shōmu. This sugoroku is not the paper picture game but ban-sugoroku, played by moving white and black pieces on a board — a relative of backgammon.","Sugoroku was tied to gambling from early on. According to the Nihon Shoki, Empress Jitō banned it in 689.","The Yōrō Code, enforced in 757, also forbade monks and nuns to play sugoroku. Bans were issued again and again, which suggests they were not obeyed.","Sugoroku stayed popular in the Heian period and appears in The Pillow Book and The Tale of Genji. The Tale of the Heike tells how the cloistered emperor Shirakawa named three things he could not control: the waters of the Kamo River, the dice of sugoroku and the warrior monks of Mount Hiei.","Edo-period prints still show young women enjoying ban-sugoroku indoors. But by the Meiji era the board game had nearly disappeared, and “sugoroku” came to mean the paper picture game."],"quiz":[["Ban-sugoroku, like the Shōsōin board, is related to which game?","Backgammon","Shogi","Go","Playing cards"],["Who banned sugoroku in 689, according to the Nihon Shoki?","Empress Jitō","Emperor Shōmu","Emperor Kanmu","Emperor Meiji"],["Shirakawa could not control the Kamo River, the warrior monks — and what?","The dice of sugoroku","Shogi pieces","Go stones","A kemari ball"]],"kf":["Ban-sugoroku is a relative of backgammon, played in Japan by the 7th century","It was banned again and again for gambling, first by Empress Jitō in 689","By the Meiji era it had faded, and “sugoroku” came to mean the paper picture game"],"more":[{"h":"The Shōsōin board","p":["The board kept in the Shōsōin is rosewood inlaid with ivory and stained woods in fine patterns. It has 24 points, twelve on each side, with flowers set on the middle divider.","The Shōsōin also keeps many sugoroku pieces of glass and stone. Ban-sugoroku uses fifteen white and fifteen black pieces and two dice — much the same kit as backgammon. The game is thought to have come from Persian nard by way of China."],"cap":["The Shōsōin sugoroku board, inlaid rosewood — play on it in the museum’s Collection room"]},{"h":"How ban-sugoroku is played","p":["Each player sets fifteen pieces in a fixed pattern and moves by the two dice. White and black travel in opposite directions; whoever gathers all their pieces into the last six points first wins.","A piece standing alone can be hit and sent back to the start. A point holding two or more cannot be entered by the opponent, so the art is to build blocks. In the museum’s Collection room you can play this game on the Shōsōin board."],"cap":["A ban-sugoroku board (diagram): the 24 points, and white and black running opposite ways"]},{"h":"Bans and fashions, round and round","p":["After Jitō’s ban sugoroku was forbidden again and again, because people kept losing everything to the gambling. Laws of the Kamakura shogunate also warn against betting on it.","Yet it was also an accomplishment of the nobility. A beautiful board went with a bride’s trousseau, and in the Edo period lavish lacquered boards were made. Hated as a gambler’s tool, loved as a treasure — ban-sugoroku had both faces."],"cap":["Ban-sugoroku in an Edo-period picture (the In-art room)"]}],"tl":[["7th century","Ban-sugoroku arrives from China"],["689","Empress Jitō bans sugoroku (Nihon shoki)"],["8th century","The inlaid rosewood board enters the Shōsōin"],["757","The Yōrō Code takes effect, warning monks and nuns off sugoroku"],["Heian period","Sugoroku appears in The Pillow Book and The Tale of Genji"],["12th century","Emperor Shirakawa’s lament over “the dice of sugoroku” (Tale of the Heike)"],["Edo period","Lavish boards are made for brides’ trousseaux"],["Meiji era","Ban-sugoroku all but stops being played"]]},"queen":{"title":"How the chess queen became the strongest piece","lead":"Today’s most powerful piece began as a counsellor who could step only one square diagonally.","s":["Chess travelled from India to Persia and the Arab world. In the Persian epic, the Shahnameh, the sage Buzurgmihr solves the riddle of chess sent from India. On those boards, the piece beside the king was the fers, the counsellor.","The fers moved only one square diagonally — a very weak piece. In the 12th-century chess set from Nishapur in Iran, it is a small throne, smaller than the king’s.","When chess reached Europe, the counsellor became a queen; the 12th-century Lewis chessmen include queens. But the new name did not change the move: she stayed weak.","The great change came in Spain at the end of the 15th century. Scachs d’amor (“Chess of love”), a poem written in Valencia around 1475, is considered the earliest record of the modern queen, moving any distance along ranks, files and diagonals. Books by Lucena (1497) and Damiano (1512) then spread the new rule across Europe.","Why the queen grew strong is not certain. Some say players wanted faster games; others suggest the influence of powerful queens such as Isabella I. Either way, the change brought chess very close to the game we play today."],"quiz":[["In Persian and Arab chess, which piece stood where the queen is now?","The fers (counsellor)","The elephant","The chariot","The knight"],["How did the original fers move?","One square diagonally","Any distance in straight lines","It jumped two squares","It could not move"],["Where was the poem with the earliest record of the modern queen written?","Valencia, 15th century","India, 6th century","Lewis, 12th century","London, 19th century"]],"kf":["The queen began as the Persian and Arab “fers” (counsellor), which could move only one square diagonally","The strong modern queen first appears in writing in Spain around 1475","Printed books spread the new rules across Europe within a few decades"],"more":[{"h":"The weak counsellor","p":["The fers could step only one square diagonally — a far cry from today’s queen, who can sweep from one edge of the board to the other. Chess in those days moved slowly, and a single game could take hours.","The elephant (today’s bishop) only leapt two squares diagonally. A pawn that reached the last rank did promote — but only into this weak fers."],"cap":["How far the fers (left) and the modern queen (right) can move"]},{"h":"“The queen’s chess” and “mad chess”","p":["Once the new queen arrived, the Spanish called the game “the queen’s chess” (ajedrez de la dama), and the Italians, for its fury, “mad chess” (scacchi alla rabbiosa). Around the same time the bishop gained the power to slide any distance diagonally.","With stronger pieces the game became fast and fierce. Books appeared on traps that win in a few moves and on set opening sequences, and chess became a game to be studied."],"cap":["Liberale da Verona, The Chess Players (about 1475) — painted just as the new queen was born"]},{"h":"Why a queen?","p":["Why the counsellor became a queen is not certain. Seeing the piece beside the king as his consort may simply have felt natural. In Latin “fers” became “fercia”, and then the piece was called “regina”, the queen.","Why she grew strong is debated too: perhaps to speed the game up, or perhaps because it was an age of powerful queens such as Isabella I of Castile. The historian Marilyn Yalom linked the change to the standing of women in the Middle Ages."],"cap":["The queen from the Lewis chessmen (12th century) — still with the old, weak move"]}],"tl":[["c. 6th century","Chaturanga, the ancestor of chess, appears in India"],["7th–9th centuries","Played in Persia and the Arab world, with the fers beside the king"],["c. 10th century","Chess reaches Europe"],["12th century","The Lewis chessmen include queens (still moving weakly)"],["c. 1475","The Valencian poem Scachs d’amor records the new queen’s move"],["1497","Lucena’s chess book is printed in Spain"],["1512","Damiano’s book appears in Italy, spreading the new rules"]]},"senet":{"title":"How senet became a journey to the afterlife","lead":"A game played in Egypt for nearly three thousand years came to stand for the soul’s journey after death.","s":["Senet is an ancient Egyptian game in which pieces move along a board of three rows of ten squares. Fragments of what may be senet boards have been found in First Dynasty tombs of about 3100 BC. Royals played on splendid boxes; ordinary people on boards scratched into stone.","Its name means “passing.” From the New Kingdom (from about 1550 BC), senet came to be linked with the journey of the dead person’s soul passing through the afterlife.","Chapter 17 of the Book of the Dead shows the dead playing senet against an invisible opponent. On the wall of the tomb of Nefertari, queen of Ramesses II, the queen sits before a senet board.","Tutankhamun’s tomb held at least five game boxes. One, a large box of ebony and ivory, has senet on top and a different twenty-square game on the other side.","Yet no full written account of the rules has been found. In the 20th century Jéquier, Bell and Kendall (1978), among others, each reconstructed a way to play from boards, pictures and fragments of texts. The Atlas’s senet is one such research-based way to play."],"quiz":[["What does the name “senet” mean?","Passing","The king’s game","Thirty houses","Crossing the river"],["In the New Kingdom, what was senet linked with?","The soul’s journey through the afterlife","Rain-making rites","Battle plans","Harvest fortunes"],["How many game boxes, at least, were found in Tutankhamun’s tomb?","Five","One","Two","A hundred"]],"kf":["One of the world’s oldest board games: pieces race over a board of 3 × 10 squares","Its name means “passing”; in the New Kingdom it became the soul’s journey through the afterlife","No rules survive; today’s ways of play are 20th-century reconstructions"],"more":[{"h":"Thirty squares and the special ones","p":["A senet board has three rows of ten — thirty squares. The pieces are thought to have run left to right along the first row, right to left along the second and left to right again along the third, tracing an S.","The last five squares were often marked with special signs. Square 26 is the “beautiful house”, 27 the “water”, and in most reconstructions a piece that falls into the water is sent back. Squares 28 to 30 bore numbers or gods of the afterlife."],"cap":["A senet board. The pieces run in an S; the last five squares (26–30) carry special signs"]},{"h":"Moving by throwing sticks","p":["Instead of dice the players threw four sticks, flat on one side and round on the other, or animal knucklebones. Many scholars think the number of flat sides up gave the move, with none up counting five.","That count is exactly the one used with the yut sticks of faraway Korea. Throw the sticks in the museum’s Chance room and see how much more often some scores come than others."],"cap":["Egyptian throwing sticks (throw them in the Chance room)"]},{"h":"Tutankhamun’s game boxes","p":["The tomb of Tutankhamun, opened by Howard Carter in 1922, held at least five game boxes. The largest is of ebony and ivory, with a drawer for the pieces and sticks.","Boxes with a board on both sides were common in this period: senet on one face, and on the other the same twenty-square game as the Royal Game of Ur. The king was meant to be able to play either game in the next world."],"cap":["Tutankhamun’s senet box — see it in 3D and play on it in the Collection room"]}],"tl":[["c. 3100 BC","Fragments of a senet-like board in a First Dynasty tomb"],["c. 2600 BC","Old Kingdom tomb paintings show people playing senet"],["From c. 1550 BC","New Kingdom: senet is tied to the journey into the afterlife"],["c. 1323 BC","Five game boxes are laid in Tutankhamun’s tomb"],["c. 1250 BC","Queen Nefertari is painted at a senet board in her tomb"],["1922","Carter opens Tutankhamun’s tomb"],["1978","Kendall publishes a reconstruction of the rules"]]},"lewis":{"title":"The chessmen that came out of the sand","lead":"Medieval chess pieces found on a Scottish island in 1831 — and one from the same workshop that turned up in a drawer 188 years later.","s":["In 1831 a hoard of pieces carved mostly from walrus ivory was found on the Isle of Lewis, off north-west Scotland: 78 chessmen, 14 discs for board games and a buckle — 93 objects in all.","Exactly where and how they were found is unclear. Uig Bay on the island’s west coast is most often named, but other places have been suggested. Who buried them there, and why, is unknown.","The pieces are thought to have been made in the later 12th century around Trondheim in Norway: kings, pensive queens, bishops, knights on stocky horses, and warders biting the rims of their shields — thought to be the berserkers of the Norse sagas.","Today 82 pieces are in the British Museum and 11 in the National Museum of Scotland. It has long been said that some pieces are missing from the complete sets.","In 2019 a warder bought for just £5 in 1964 by an Edinburgh antiques dealer, and kept in a family drawer, was recognised as coming from the Lewis chessmen workshop. It sold at Sotheby’s for £735,000."],"quiz":[["In what year were the pieces found on Lewis?","1831","1066","1931","1700"],["What are the pieces mostly made of?","Walrus ivory","Marble","Gold","Glass"],["When was the warder sold in 2019 first bought for £5?","1964","1831","1990","2018"]],"kf":["93 pieces and discs found on the Isle of Lewis, Scotland, in 1831","Carved mostly from walrus ivory in Norway in the later 12th century","Now split between the British Museum and National Museums Scotland"],"more":[{"h":"The line-up of 78 chessmen","p":["The 78 chessmen are 8 kings, 8 queens, 16 bishops, 15 knights, 12 warders and 19 pawns — parts of at least four sets.","The bishops wear mitres. The change of the chess “elephant” into a bishop happened in Europe, and the Lewis pieces are among its earliest examples. Some warders bite their shields, and are thought to show berserkers — warriors who fought in a frenzy."],"cap":["The Lewis chessmen (see them in 3D in the Collection room)","The 78 chessmen by kind"]},{"h":"Walrus ivory and the northern seas","p":["Most pieces are of walrus ivory, a few of whale’s tooth. Walrus ivory was a treasure of the northern seas, shipped from Greenland and northern Norway.","Lewis then belonged to the islands ruled by the king of Norway; it passed to Scotland by the Treaty of Perth in 1266. Fragments carved in the same way have been found near Trondheim Cathedral — a clue to where the pieces were made."],"cap":[]},{"h":"The legend of the find, and fame today","p":["There are many stories of who found them: a cow that scraped them out of the sand, or a local man, Malcolm MacLeod. The finder soon sold them, and by way of Edinburgh the British Museum bought most of the hoard.","With their glum and comic faces the pieces are hugely popular, and replicas sell all over the world. The wizard’s chess in the film Harry Potter and the Philosopher’s Stone (2001) used pieces based on the Lewis chessmen."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["Late 12th century","The pieces are made in Norway (around Trondheim)"],["1266","The Treaty of Perth hands the isles, Lewis among them, to Scotland"],["1831","The 93 pieces are found together on Lewis"],["1831–32","The British Museum buys 82"],["1888","The Scottish national museum acquires 11"],["2001","The pieces appear in the wizard’s chess of the Harry Potter film"],["2019","A warder bought for £5 sells for £735,000"]]},"tafl":{"title":"The king’s escape and a mistaken translation","lead":"How to play the Vikings’ board game was worked out from an 18th-century travel diary from Lapland.","s":["In Viking-age northern Europe people played board games called tafl. A king in the centre and his few defenders fight a larger army surrounding them — a game of two unequal sides.","A 10th-century wooden board found at Ballinderry in Ireland in 1932 has 7×7 holes, with the centre and the four corners marked: thought to be where the king stands and where he escapes to.","But almost no medieval account of the rules survives. A key clue is the diary of the 25-year-old naturalist Linnaeus from his journey through Lapland (northern Sweden) in 1732. There he wrote down how the Sámi played a game called tablut.","The diary was published in English translation in 1811, but the translation contained mistakes. Murray’s reconstruction (1913), based on it, became the standard tafl rules for many years.","Players and researchers later corrected the error, and today many versions of the rules are played. The Atlas’s tafl is one of them."],"quiz":[["What game did Linnaeus record in Lapland?","Tablut","Senet","Mancala","Chess"],["Which piece do the defenders try to bring to a corner?","The king","The queen","A flag","A ship"],["When was the English translation with the mistake published?","1811","1732","1932","1066"]],"kf":["A king and a few defenders in the centre, surrounded by a larger army — a game of two unequal sides","Played in Viking-age northern Europe, on boards from 7×7 up to 19×19","The rules come down to us through Linnaeus’s 1732 travel diary — and its flawed English translation"],"more":[{"h":"The Ballinderry board","p":["Found in a lake dwelling (crannog) in the Irish midlands, the Ballinderry board is made of yew. Its 49 holes in a 7×7 grid probably held pieces with pegs on their bases.","A handle carved as a human head sticks out from the frame — this was a board you could carry. Irish sources name a 7×7 tafl game, brandubh (“black raven”), which may well have been played on it."],"cap":["The Ballinderry board (7×7) set up for brandubh, an Irish tafl game. The gold rings mark the centre and the four corners"]},{"h":"Linnaeus’s tablut","p":["The tablut Linnaeus recorded uses a 9×9 board. The king stands on the central throne; his 8 “Swedes” face 16 “Muscovites”.","Every piece moves any distance in a straight line, like a rook, and is captured by being sandwiched between two enemies. The defenders win if the king reaches the edge; the attackers win by surrounding him on all four sides. Linnaeus wrote in a mix of Latin and Swedish, and several points shifted in the English translation."],"cap":["Tablut as Linnaeus recorded it (9×9): the king on the central throne, 8 defenders, 16 attackers. The defenders win if the king reaches a green edge square"]},{"h":"Growing boards, a fading game","p":["Besides 7×7 there were 9×9 boards, the Welsh 11×11, and even 19×19. The largest, Alea Evangelii, is drawn in a 12th-century manuscript that says it came from the court of King Æthelstan of England in the 10th century.","As chess spread through northern Europe in the 12th and 13th centuries, tafl slowly fell out of use. Only in distant Lapland did tablut survive — and Linnaeus happened to write it down. Today international tournaments are played on 11×11 boards, and the game is winning new players."],"cap":["The Lewis hoard (12th century): alongside the chessmen were 14 discs for a board game"]}],"tl":[["10th c.","The Ballinderry board is made in Ireland"],["924–939","Alea Evangelii is said to be played at King Æthelstan’s court"],["12th c.","Alea Evangelii is drawn in a manuscript"],["12th–13th c.","Chess spreads in northern Europe and tafl fades away"],["1732","Linnaeus records tablut in Lapland"],["1811","Lachesis Lapponica, the English translation of his diary"],["1913","Murray reconstructs tafl in A History of Chess"],["1932","The Ballinderry board is found"]]},"goose":{"title":"The Game of the Goose, a gift for a king","lead":"A dice race around a spiral of 63 squares was given to a king — and then played all over Europe.","s":["The Game of the Goose is a race game: throw the dice and move around a spiral of 63 squares to the goal in the centre. Land on a goose and you move on again by the same number. The well, the prison and Death hold you back or send you home.","It is thought to have begun in 16th-century Italy. Francesco I de’ Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany, is said to have given the game to King Philip II of Spain sometime between 1574 and 1587.","In England, the London printer John Wolfe registered “the newe and most pleasant game of the goose” in 1597. In the 17th and 18th centuries it was hugely popular across Europe.","Printed on paper, it spread in countless versions with new pictures: boards for learning geography, boards of stories and fables, even advertising boards. It is thought to be the model for many later race games.","A double-sided board in the Metropolitan Museum of Art has the goose game on one side and chess on the other. Thought to be made in the later 16th century by craftsmen in Gujarat, India, it may be the oldest surviving goose board."],"quiz":[["How many squares does the Game of the Goose have?","63","64","100","30"],["Who is said to have given the game to the King of Spain?","Francesco I de’ Medici","Napoleon","Elizabeth I","Louis XIV"],["What happens when you land on a goose?","You move on again by the same number","You go back to the start","You miss a turn","You swap places with a rival"]],"kf":["A race along a spiral of 63 squares to the goal in the centre, by the throw of the dice","Born in 16th-century Italy and, it is said, sent by the Medici to the King of Spain","Spread as printed sheets, in countless versions with new pictures and themes"],"more":[{"h":"A spiral of 63 squares","p":["In the best-known version there are a few special squares besides the geese. The bridge on 6 carries you to 12; at the inn on 19 you miss a turn; in the well on 31 and the prison on 52 you are stuck until another player comes to free you.","The maze on 42 sends you back to 30, and death on 58 sends you back to the start. You must land on 63 with the exact throw — any extra and you bounce back. The last step is the hardest of all."],"cap":["The 63 squares laid out as a spiral from the outside in. Green: geese; blue: the bridge; yellow: hold-ups (inn, well, prison); red: setbacks (maze, death); gold: the goal"]},{"h":"The puzzle of nine","p":["The geese stand on 5, 9, 14, 18… at alternate gaps of 4 and 5. Every multiple of 9 has a goose, so a 9 landing on 9 would carry you on to 18, 27… and straight to 63.","That is why versions played with two dice have a special rule: a first throw of 6 and 3 goes to 26, and 5 and 4 goes to 53. The number 63 is 7×9, and it has been linked to the age of 63, then thought the most dangerous year of a life."],"cap":["Geese stand every 4 and 5 squares, so there is always a goose on a multiple of 9. A first throw of 9 would hop from goose to goose straight to 63"]},{"h":"Spread by the printing press","p":["Boards were printed from copper plates and woodblocks and became cheap to buy. In France it was the jeu de l’oie, in Spain the juego de la oca, where a rhyme about hopping “from goose to goose” is still a common saying.","New versions with new pictures kept appearing: boards for learning geography and history, boards about the French Revolution or Napoleon, even boards advertising products. On the same 63-square track, each age drew its own concerns."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["16th c.","Thought to be born in Italy"],["1574–87","Francesco I de’ Medici is said to send it to Philip II of Spain"],["late 16th c.","A double-sided board, goose on one side and chess on the other, is made in Gujarat, India"],["1597","John Wolfe registers it for printing in London"],["17th–18th c.","A craze across Europe, with ever more themed versions"],["1790s","Boards about the French Revolution are made"],["19th c.","Printed in huge numbers in many countries as a children’s game"]]},"shogi":{"title":"When did shogi’s dropped pieces begin?","lead":"Of all the chess family, only shogi lets you put captured pieces back on the board as your own. When — and why — did that rule appear?","s":["Chess’s Indian ancestor spread east and west; in Japan it became shogi. Japan’s oldest shogi pieces are 16 found in excavations in the early 1990s on the old grounds of Kōfuku-ji in Nara. A label dated 1058 (Tenki 6) was found with them, so the pieces are thought to be from about the same time.","Writings of the period mention shogi too. The Shin Sarugaku-ki of the mid-11th century names it, and the Nichūreki, which gathers records from the end of the Heian period, describes “Heian shogi” with kings, golds, silvers, knights, lances and pawns.","In the Kamakura and Muromachi periods many larger forms with more pieces appeared. The “small shogi” behind today’s game added the rook and bishop — and a piece called the drunk elephant, which no longer exists.","The drop rule is thought to have appeared around the 16th century. Tradition says Emperor Go-Nara had the drunk elephant removed from small shogi, and the drops are thought to have begun at about that time.","Why drops arose is not clear. One view: both sides’ pieces are the same colour, told apart only by the way they point, so a captured piece is easy to reuse. Thanks to drops, shogi keeps its pieces to the very end and rarely ends in a draw."],"quiz":[["What date was written on the label found with the Kōfuku-ji pieces?","1058 (Tenki 6)","701 (Taihō 1)","1582 (Tenshō 10)","1868 (Meiji 1)"],["When is the drop rule thought to have appeared?","Around the 16th century","Around the 8th century","Around the 19th century","In the 20th century"],["What is given as a reason captured pieces were easy to reuse?","Both sides’ pieces are the same colour, told apart by direction","The pieces were made of gold","The board had few squares","Pieces were chosen by dice"]],"kf":["A chess relative born in India that grew up in Japan, played on a 9×9 board","The oldest pieces come from the old Kōfukuji precinct, found with a tag dated 1058","“Pieces in hand” — dropping captured pieces as your own — probably began around the 16th century"],"more":[{"h":"One colour, told apart by direction","p":["Chess pieces are painted white and black, but shogi pieces are all the same natural wood. You tell whose they are by which way the point of the five-sided piece faces. That shape is what lets a captured piece simply be turned around and dropped back in.","Each piece has its name written on it, and on entering the enemy camp it can turn over and “promote”. A pawn becomes the と on its back and moves like a gold general. Flat, lettered pieces rather than carved figures are something shogi shares with its East Asian cousins, Chinese xiangqi and Korean janggi."],"cap":["Shogi pieces are the same colour for both sides and are told apart by which way the point faces. Right: a pawn turned over, “promoted” (tokin)"]},{"h":"The age of giant shogi","p":["From the Kamakura and Muromachi periods into the Edo period, larger and larger forms of shogi were made, with ever more kinds of pieces. The 12×12 chū shogi was widely played through the Edo period and still has fans today.","Records go further still: 15×15 dai shogi, 25×25 tai shogi, and taikyoku shogi with 804 pieces on a 36×36 board. Yet the everyday game was the 9×9 shō shogi — which gained pieces in hand and became the shogi we play now."],"cap":["Board sizes compared, drawn from one corner: 9×9 is modern shogi (40 pieces), 12×12 chū shogi (92), 15×15 dai shogi (130), 25×25 tai shogi (354), 36×36 taikyoku shogi (804)"]},{"h":"Shogi in Edo","p":["In the Edo period, shogi houses were paid stipends by the shogunate; Ōhashi Sōkei is counted as the first master. Each year the best players met before the shogun in the “castle games”, later fixed for 17 November — the origin of today’s Shogi Day.","Shogi was a pleasure for townspeople too: Harunobu’s print shows a young couple playing beside a mosquito net. Collections of tsume-shogi problems as fine as works of art also appeared, such as Itō Kanju’s Shōgi Zukō (1755)."],"cap":["A colour woodblock print by Suzuki Harunobu (c. 1767–69): a young woman and man playing shogi beside a mosquito net"]}],"tl":[["1058","Pieces from the old Kōfukuji precinct (found with a tag dated Tengi 6)"],["mid-11th c.","Shogi is named in the Shinsarugakuki"],["early Kamakura","The Nichūreki lists the pieces of Heian shogi"],["c. 16th c.","Pieces in hand are thought to begin"],["1612","Ōhashi Sōkei and others are said to receive stipends from the shogunate"],["1716","The castle games are fixed for 17 November"],["1755","Itō Kanju’s Shōgi Zukō"],["1975","17 November becomes Shogi Day"]]},"pachisi":{"title":"How pachisi became Ludo","lead":"A cross-shaped board game played at Indian courts changed its name and reached playrooms all over the world.","s":["India has games called chaupar and pachisi, in which four players move pieces round a cross-shaped board. Chaupar uses long dice (pasa); pachisi uses thrown cowrie shells. The name pachisi means “twenty-five,” the highest score the cowries could give.","Even the gods played. A painting from the end of the 17th century shows the god Shiva and his wife Parvati around a chaupar board on a tiger skin.","At Fatehpur Sikri, the city of the Mughal emperor Akbar (16th century), a courtyard still has a huge cross-shaped board laid out in its floor. Akbar is said to have played with people in coloured clothes as the pieces.","In the 19th century the game crossed the seas. In America it was sold as Parcheesi, trademarked in 1874. In Britain it was sold in the 1890s as Ludo — Latin for “I play” — and became a family favourite.","Ludo is simpler: one die, and a 6 to bring a piece out. Relatives under other names are played all over the world, such as Parchís in Spain and Mensch ärgere Dich nicht (“Don’t get annoyed”) in Germany."],"quiz":[["What does the name “pachisi” mean?","Twenty-five","The king’s road","Cross","Cowrie shell"],["Which of Akbar’s cities still has a courtyard laid out as a huge board?","Fatehpur Sikri","The Qutb Minar in Delhi","The Taj Mahal","Ellora"],["What does “Ludo” mean in Latin?","I play","I win","I run","I throw"]],"kf":["A race game on a cross-shaped Indian board, four players in two teams bringing their pieces round","The name means “25” — the biggest throw of the cowrie shells","In the 19th century it travelled abroad and spread worldwide as Parcheesi, Ludo and more"],"more":[{"h":"The cross and the road","p":["A pachisi board is a cross with four arms reaching out from the central charkoni. Each arm is 3 × 8 squares, 96 squares in all. Boards were often embroidered on cloth, so they could be rolled up and put away.","Pieces leave the centre, go down the middle column of their own arm, travel once round the board anticlockwise, and come back up the middle column to the centre. Players sitting opposite are partners, and a team wins when both have brought all four pieces home."],"cap":["A cross-shaped board (each arm 3 × 8 squares). The blue line is one player’s road: down the middle of their own arm, anticlockwise all the way round, and back up the middle to the centre"]},{"h":"Counting cowries","p":["In pachisi six cowrie shells are thrown, and the number landing mouth up decides the move. Two to six up move that many squares; but one up moves 10, and none up moves 25.","At 1 in 64, 25 is the rarest and biggest throw — and it gave the game its name. A 6, 10 or 25 earns another throw. Chaupar instead uses three long stick dice marked 1, 2, 5 and 6."],"cap":["Of six cowries, how many land mouth up (top row) and how far that moves you (bottom row). Bar heights show how often each happens if every shell falls either way equally"]},{"h":"New names, new countries","p":["Through the British in India, the game reached Europe and America in the 19th century. In the US it became the trademark Parcheesi; in Britain Ludo was patented in 1896, with simpler rules and a single cube die in place of the cowries.","Germany got Mensch ärgere dich nicht (“Don’t get annoyed”) in 1914, and Spain plays parchís. Names and details differ from country to country, but the frame is the same: round the cross and home again."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["16th c.","A courtyard with a giant cross board is laid out at Akbar’s Fatehpur Sikri"],["17th–18th c.","Indian miniatures often show people gathered around chaupar"],["1694–95","Devidasa paints Shiva and Parvati playing chaupar"],["1874","Parcheesi becomes a trademark in the US"],["1896","Ludo is patented in Britain"],["1914","Mensch ärgere dich nicht goes on sale in Germany"]]},"go":{"title":"What a 1,300-year-old go board tells us","lead":"The go board kept in the Shōsōin has 19 lines like today’s — and 17 flower marks, unlike today’s.","s":["Go began in ancient China. Legend says the sage king Yao made it to teach his son. The book of Mencius, from before the common era, names a master player, Yi Qiu.","The size of the board is thought to have changed over time: boards of 17 lines were also used in early times, and the 19-line board of today spread later.","When go reached Japan is not clear, but it was played by the Nara period. The rules for monks and nuns banned music and gambling, yet allowed go and the koto — very different from the treatment of sugoroku.","The Shōsōin’s marquetry go board has come down as a treasure linked to Emperor Shōmu. It has 19 lines like today’s board, but 17 flower marks where today’s board has 9 star points. Its two drawers are linked so that pulling one brings out the other. The Shōsōin also keeps go stones of ivory dyed red and dark blue, carved with birds.","In the Heian period The Tale of Genji shows people playing go. In the Edo period houses such as the Hon’inbō served the shogunate, and go became an art in which professional players tested their skill."],"quiz":[["How many lines does the Shōsōin go board have?","19","17","13","9"],["What did the rules for monks and nuns allow them to play?","Go and the koto","Sugoroku","Gambling games","Kemari"],["How many flower marks does the Shōsōin board have instead of star points?","17","9","4","25"]],"kf":["Black and white stones on the points where lines cross, competing for the larger territory — a game born in ancient China","After an age of 17-line boards came 19 lines; the Shōsōin keeps an 8th-century 19-line board","In Edo Japan, go houses served the shogunate; in the 21st century an AI beat the world’s top player"],"more":[{"h":"Surround to capture, enclose to win","p":["In go, Black and White take turns placing stones on the points where the lines cross. Once placed, a stone never moves. When every empty point next to a stone (or group) is filled by the opponent, it is captured and removed from the board.","At the end, the territory you have walled in plus the stones you have taken decides the winner. The rules are few, but the board is wide: the number of possible positions on a 19-line board has been calculated at about 10 to the power 170."],"cap":["How stones are captured. Left: the black stone has four empty points beside it (red). Middle: White closes in on three sides, leaving one. Right: the last is filled and the black stone is taken"]},{"h":"From 17 lines to 19","p":["A 17-line stone go board was found in an Eastern Han tomb at Wangdu in Hebei, China. The Dunhuang Classic of Go, thought to date from around the 6th century, describes 361 points — a 19-line board. The board seems to have grown in between.","The Shōsōin board has 19 lines, but instead of today’s nine star points it has flower marks in 17 places. Going from 289 points to 361 made the game far deeper."],"cap":["The 17-line and 19-line boards compared by number of points. The nine black dots on today’s board are the “star points”"]},{"h":"Go of the immortals, go of the masters","p":["A Chinese tale tells of a woodcutter who stopped in the mountains to watch immortals playing go — and found that so many years had passed that his axe handle had rotted away. Go as a pastime of immortals was a favourite subject for dishes and paintings.","In Edo Japan, go houses such as the Hon’inbō were paid by the shogunate and go was polished into an art. Hon’inbō Shūsai’s retirement game of 1938 became Kawabata Yasunari’s novel The Master of Go. In 2016 the AI AlphaGo beat the world’s leading player, Lee Sedol, four games to one, astonishing the world."],"cap":["A carved lacquer dish with immortals playing go (Ming dynasty, Xuande period, 1425–36)"]}],"tl":[["c. 4th c. BC","Mencius names the go master Yi Qiu"],["Eastern Han","A 17-line stone board is placed in a tomb at Wangdu"],["c. 6th c.","The Dunhuang Classic of Go describes 19 lines and 361 points"],["8th c.","The Shōsōin go board of sandalwood with marquetry"],["1612","Hon’inbō Sansa and others are said to receive stipends from the shogunate"],["1938","Hon’inbō Shūsai’s retirement game, later Kawabata’s The Master of Go"],["2016","AlphaGo beats Lee Sedol four games to one"]]},"draughts":{"title":"How checkers was born","lead":"An Arab board game, the chessboard and the chess queen: checkers is thought to have grown from the meeting of the three.","s":["The ancestor of checkers is thought to be alquerque, played on a 5×5 grid of points joined by lines. In Arabic it was called qirq and is named in the 10th-century Book of Songs. The Moors brought it to Spain, and its rules appear in the Book of Games made for King Alfonso X of Castile in the 13th century.","Around the 12th century, probably in southern France, people began moving alquerque’s pieces on a chessboard. This was fierges. The pieces took the name of the chess queen of the day, the fers.","Promotion — a piece growing stronger when it reaches the far side — is already recorded in a chronicle of the mid-13th century. It may have been borrowed from the chess pawn becoming a queen.","The great change came in France around 1535: compulsory capture was added, so that if you can take, you must. The game was called the jeu forcé, the “forced game.” English draughts and American checkers come down from it.","The old game without forced capture lived on as a “gentle game for ladies” and later led to the 10×10 international game. From one ancestor branched many “games of dames,” of queens."],"quiz":[["Which game is thought to be the ancestor of checkers?","Alquerque","Senet","Liubo","Mancala"],["When and where was compulsory capture added?","France, around 1535","Baghdad, 10th century","America, 1900","Japan, 8th century"],["The pieces of fierges took the name of what?","The chess queen (fers)","The chess king","The dice","The knight"]],"kf":["Born when the pieces of the Arabic game alquerque were moved onto a chessboard","Pieces move diagonally and capture by jumping; reach the far row and they promote","Compulsory capture came in around 1535, and in 2007 the game was solved"],"more":[{"h":"From points and lines to the chessboard","p":["An alquerque board is 25 points in a 5 × 5 grid joined by lines. Each side sets out 12 pieces, and play begins from the one empty point in the middle. Pieces move along the lines and capture by jumping over an enemy next to them.","When the pieces moved onto a chessboard, only the squares of one colour were used. An 8 × 8 board has 32 dark squares; fill the first three rows on each side and you have 12 pieces each — the same number as alquerque."],"cap":["The alquerque board and its starting position: 5 × 5 points joined by lines across and diagonally, 12 pieces a side, only the centre empty"]},{"h":"Jump to capture, crown to grow strong","p":["Men move one square diagonally forward and capture by jumping an enemy next to them if the square beyond is empty. If another capture is then possible, they keep jumping. In today’s checkers, if you can capture, you must.","A man that reaches the far row is crowned — a second piece is stacked on it — and as a king it may move backwards too. In the international game on a 10 × 10 board, kings fly any distance along a diagonal, which transforms the endgame."],"cap":["Capturing and crowning in checkers. The red man jumps two white men in a row (×), and later reaches the far row to become a king, two pieces stacked"]},{"h":"A solved game and a spreading family","p":["In Canada, Jonathan Schaeffer and his team built the checkers program Chinook. In 1992 it played Marion Tinsley, who had barely lost a game in over 40 years, and Tinsley won. The team kept computing, and in 2007 they proved that with perfect play by both sides, checkers is a draw.","Meanwhile the family descended from the same ancestor lives on worldwide: international draughts on 10 × 10, Russian shashki, Turkish dama with its orthogonal moves, Canadian checkers on 12 × 12 — each still played in tournaments today."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["10th c.","The Book of Songs names qirq (alquerque)"],["c. 12th c.","Fierges is born, probably in southern France"],["1283","Alfonso X’s Book of Games describes alquerque"],["c. 1535","Compulsory capture is added in France"],["1547","Torquemada in Spain publishes the first book on draughts"],["18th c.","The 10 × 10 board spreads in France and the Netherlands"],["1992","Tinsley beats the program Chinook"],["2007","Checkers is proved a draw with perfect play"]]},"reversi":{"title":"Reversi and Othello: who invented them?","lead":"A game of trapping and turning black and white discs — and two men in 19th-century England who each claimed it.","s":["In 1880s London two Englishmen, Lewis Waterman and John W. Mollett, each claimed to be the game’s inventor and called the other a fraud.","Mollett’s game, Annexation, was played on a cross-shaped board. Waterman’s Reversi used an ordinary 8×8 board, and it is his form that survives. The quarrel over the name even went to court.","Reversi spread through Europe; in 1893 the German firm Ravensburger made it one of its first products. In Japan it was described in Meiji-era books and was also called Genpei-go.","Its popularity did not last. In 1971 Goro Hasegawa launched “Othello,” fixing the opening with the first four discs crossed in the centre, and it became a craze in Japan.","The name is said to come from Shakespeare’s play, whose black general Othello and his white wife Desdemona echo the black and white discs. Today’s tournament rules are those of Othello."],"quiz":[["The two men who quarrelled over Reversi lived when and where?","19th-century England","18th-century France","20th-century Japan","16th-century Italy"],["What shape was the board of Mollett’s Annexation?","A cross","A circle","A triangle","An 8×8 square"],["Who launched Othello in 1971?","Goro Hasegawa","Hon’inbō Shūsaku","Lewis Waterman","Ravensburger"]],"kf":["Trap your opponent’s discs between your own to turn them over; whoever has more discs at the end wins","In 1880s London, two Englishmen fought over who had invented it","It became a craze in Japan in the 1970s as Othello, and in 2023 it was shown to be a draw with perfect play"],"more":[{"h":"Bracket and flip","p":["You may only place a disc where it traps a line of your opponent’s discs between it and one of your own. Lines run in eight directions — across, up and down, and diagonally — and one move can trap several at once. Every trapped disc is turned over to your colour.","If you have no legal move you pass, and the game ends when neither player can move. Corner discs can never be trapped, so taking a corner is a big advantage. Strong players care less about the number of discs now than about limiting where the opponent can play."],"cap":["How discs flip. Black placed on the square ringed in red brackets white in three directions — across, down and diagonally — and all seven gold-rimmed discs turn black"]},{"h":"The first four discs","p":["In early reversi, the two players took turns placing the first four discs on the four central squares, so the opening could be side by side or crosswise.","Othello fixed the crosswise start. From the first move both colours are easy to trap, so the fight begins at once. World championships today start from this position."],"cap":["The first four discs. Left: one way early reversi could begin, with the same colours side by side. Right: the crosswise start fixed by Othello"]},{"h":"Humans and computers","p":["Othello spread from Japan to the world, and the first World Othello Championship was held in Tokyo in 1977. The rules are simple, so depth of reading makes the difference; strong players see dozens of moves ahead.","In 1997 the program Logistello played world champion Takeshi Murakami six games and won all six. In 2023 computation showed that 8 × 8 Othello is a draw when both sides play perfectly."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1880s","Waterman and Mollett fight over the invention in London"],["1893","Ravensburger in Germany brings out Reversi"],["Meiji era","Japanese books introduce it as genpei-go"],["1973","Othello sets go on sale in Japan"],["1977","First World Othello Championship, in Tokyo"],["1997","Logistello beats world champion Takeshi Murakami 6–0"],["2023","8 × 8 Othello is shown to be a draw with perfect play"]]},"mancala":{"title":"Where did mancala begin?","lead":"A game of sowing stones from pit to pit, played across Africa and Asia under hundreds of names.","s":["Mancala is the family name for games in which you lift stones or seeds from a pit and sow them one by one into the pits ahead. The word is said to come from the Arabic naqala, “to move.”","Names and rules vary from place to place — oware in West Africa, bao in East Africa, mangala in Turkey, congkak in Southeast Asia, and hundreds more — with two rows or four, and any number of pits.","It is often called extremely old, but the firm evidence is younger than you might think. The oldest securely dated boards so far are pottery and stone boards of about the 6th–7th centuries from sites of the ancient Kingdom of Aksum, at Matara and Yeha in Eritrea and Ethiopia.","Older examples have been claimed, but whether those rows of holes were really for mancala is uncertain, and scholars disagree. A leading view is that the game spread from Aksum to Arabia and inland Africa.","Full written accounts of how it was played appear only around the 17th century. In the Atlas you can play mancala by eight rule sets, including oware and mangala."],"quiz":[["What does the Arabic word behind “mancala” mean?","To move","Seed","Pit","King"],["Where were the oldest securely dated mancala boards found?","Sites of the ancient Kingdom of Aksum (Ethiopia and Eritrea)","The Colosseum in Rome","The Great Wall of China","Stonehenge in England"],["Which mancala game is played in West Africa?","Oware","Liubo","Tafl","Pachisi"]],"kf":["A family of “sowing” games: lift the seeds from a pit and drop them one by one into the pits that follow","The oldest securely dated boards come from the ancient kingdom of Aksum, around the 6th–7th century","Two rows or four, with or without stores — the board and rules vary from region to region"],"more":[{"h":"Sowing","p":["In mancala you choose a pit on your side, lift out all its seeds, and drop them one at a time into the pits that follow. This movement, like scattering seed in a field, is called sowing.","Oware, from West Africa, begins with 48 seeds, four in each of 12 pits. If your last seed makes two or three in an opponent’s pit, you capture them. Knowing where a sowing will end — counting ahead — is everything. Oware crossed the Atlantic and is also played in the Caribbean."],"cap":["Sowing in oware. The three seeds of the gold-ringed pit go one by one anticlockwise; the last makes exactly 3 in an opponent’s pit, so those 3 are captured (red ring)"]},{"h":"Two rows and four","p":["Mancala boards fall broadly into two-row and four-row types. Two-row boards are common in West Africa and Asia; four-row boards spread from East Africa to southern Africa. Tanzania’s bao has 4 rows of 8 pits; its name is simply Swahili for “board”.","Some, like congkak and sungka in Southeast Asia, have a large pit at each end — a store — for captured seeds. Kalah, born in 20th-century America, is a two-row game with stores, and in 2000 computation showed that Kalah with six pits and four seeds each is a win for the first player."],"cap":["Examples of mancala board shapes: 2 rows of 6 (oware and others), 2 rows of 7 with a store at each end (congkak in Southeast Asia and others), 4 rows of 8 (bao in East Africa and others)"]},{"h":"Those who wrote it down","p":["The first European scholar to describe mancala in detail is usually said to be Thomas Hyde of England. In De Ludis Orientalibus (1694) he gave its Arabic name and how it was played.","In 1836 Edward W. Lane, describing everyday life in Cairo, recorded “mankalah” as it was played in coffee houses. Today researchers have gathered games from all over, and the recorded rule sets number in the hundreds."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["6th–7th c.","The oldest securely dated boards, from sites of ancient Aksum"],["1694","Thomas Hyde describes mancala in De Ludis Orientalibus"],["18th–19th c.","Oware and others cross the Atlantic to the Caribbean"],["1836","E. W. Lane records mankalah in Cairo"],["mid-20th c.","Kalah, a two-row game with stores, goes on sale in the US"],["2000","Kalah with six pits and four seeds is shown to be a first-player win"]]},"morris":{"title":"Nine men’s morris is everywhere","lead":"A temple roof, a Viking ship, a cathedral’s stone bench: its lines have been cut wherever people sat together.","s":["In nine men’s morris each player places nine pieces on the 24 points of three nested squares; three in a row, a “mill”, takes an enemy piece. “Morris” has nothing to do with the dance: it is said to come from the Latin merellus, a counter or token.","A board cut into a roof slab of the temple at Kurna in Egypt is often called the oldest, from about 1400 BC. But the scholar Friedrich Berger noted Coptic crosses among the same carvings and concluded that they cannot be dated. Securely dated boards begin in Roman times.","On the Gokstad ship from Norway, about AD 900, a board is cut into a deck plank. In England boards survive on the stone benches of cathedral cloisters such as Canterbury and Salisbury.","Alfonso X’s Book of Games of 1283 even shows it played with dice. Shakespeare, in A Midsummer Night’s Dream, has a wet season leave “the nine men’s morris” filled up with mud — a board dug into the village green.","In 1996 the German Ralph Gasser had a computer search every position and proved that perfect play on both sides ends in a draw. Between two people it is still a very good game."],"quiz":[["Where is the name “morris” said to come from?","The Latin for a counter","An English dance","A person’s name","A ship’s name"],["What did scholars point out about the Kurna temple boards?","They cannot be dated","They are made of gold","They came from China","They belonged to an emperor"],["What did a computer prove in 1996?","Perfect play ends in a draw","The first player always wins","The second player always wins","Eight pieces are enough"]],"kf":["Nine pieces each on the 24 points of three nested squares; make three in a row to take an enemy piece","Since Roman times its board has been cut wherever people gathered — ship decks, cathedral benches","In 1996 every position was examined by computer: with perfect play it is a draw"],"more":[{"h":"Place, move, fly","p":["The game runs in three phases. First the players take turns placing their nine pieces on empty points. When all are placed, they move one piece at a time along a line to a neighbouring empty point.","Three of your pieces in a row along a line make a “mill”, and you take one of your opponent’s pieces from the board. A common rule lets a player reduced to three pieces “fly” to any empty point. You lose when you are down to two pieces or cannot move."],"cap":["A nine men’s morris board. White has made a mill of three along the outer edge (gold) and may take one black piece (red cross)"]},{"h":"From three men to twelve","p":["The family is named by its number of pieces. Three men’s morris, with three pieces each, is a small board close to noughts and crosses. Six men’s morris uses a board of two nested squares.","Twelve men’s morris adds diagonal lines to the nine men’s board. In South Africa it is called morabaraba; herd boys are said to have scratched it on the ground, and today it has its own tournaments."],"cap":["Boards compared. The number is how many pieces each player has: three, six, nine and twelve men’s morris"]},{"h":"A chess champion and a computer","p":["The world chess champion Emanuel Lasker was among those taken with the game. In his book Brettspiele der Völker (1931) he proposed a version with ten pieces each, in which pieces may move even before all are placed.","In 1996 Ralph Gasser in Switzerland combined complete tables of endgame positions with a search from the opening to show that nine men’s morris is a draw when both sides play perfectly. The board has only 24 points, yet the positions run into billions."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["Roman era","Boards are cut in stone across the empire (the first secure examples)"],["c. 900","A board is cut on a deck plank of the Gokstad ship in Norway"],["1283","Alfonso X’s Book of Games illustrates morris"],["Middle Ages","Boards are cut on cloister benches of English cathedrals"],["1590s","Shakespeare’s A Midsummer Night’s Dream mentions a board filled with mud"],["1931","Lasker proposes a new version in Brettspiele der Völker"],["1996","Gasser proves a draw with perfect play"]]},"patolli":{"title":"Patolli, the Aztec bean race","lead":"A cross-shaped board and beans for dice: a game whose gambling ran so hot that the Spanish banned it.","s":["Patolli was a race game of the Aztecs of Mexico. Its name means beans: players threw five red beans marked or drilled on one side and moved by the number of marks showing, around a cross-shaped track painted on a mat or the ground.","Diego Durán, recording the region in the 16th century, wrote that players burned incense and prayed to the beans before throwing, and called on Macuilxochitl, the god of games, for a win.","Nobles staked jewels, cloaks, even themselves; some who lost became slaves. A story also tells of the emperor Moctezuma watching his nobles play.","Spanish friars, disliking the gambling and the prayers to the old gods, banned patolli, broke the mats and burned the beans, so few sets survive. Yet boards cut into stone floors and rocks have been found at Teotihuacan and Maya sites, and in 1977 among rock carvings in Jalisco.","A cross-shaped board with pieces running round it looks very like India’s pachisi. In 1878 the Englishman E. B. Tylor argued for an Asian origin; today most think the two arose separately."],"quiz":[["What did patolli players throw instead of dice?","Marked beans","Animal bones","Shells","Wooden sticks"],["Why, according to the records, was patolli banned?","Gambling and prayers to the old gods","The board was too big","Beans were too costly","The king lost"],["Which game is patolli’s board very like?","Pachisi","Go","Checkers","Mancala"]],"kf":["An Aztec race game: throw five marked beans and move pieces round a cross-shaped board","Bound up with gambling and prayer to the gods, it was banned by the Spanish friars","It looks strikingly like India’s pachisi, but the two are thought to have arisen separately"],"more":[{"h":"A cross-shaped road of 52 squares","p":["A patolli board is a cross-shaped track drawn on cloth, a mat or a stone floor. Reconstructions usually give each of the four arms two lanes of squares, which with the four centre squares makes 52.","Fifty-two is also the number of years in the Aztec calendar round, and some researchers see the board as a picture of the calendar or of passing time. Players took their pieces once round the track, and the first home won."],"cap":["A patolli board as often reconstructed: four arms of two lanes of six squares, plus four centre squares, 52 in all. The red squares at the arm ends are thought to be special"]},{"h":"Five beans","p":["Instead of dice, players threw five beans marked with a hole or a sign on one side and moved as many squares as marks showed. All five marks come up only once in 32 throws.","Before throwing, players are said to have spoken to the beans and called on Macuilxochitl, the god of games. The 16th-century Codex Magliabechiano shows people around a patolli board under the god’s eye."],"cap":["Of five beans, how many land with the marked side up (0–5) and how often (out of 32), if each falls either way equally"]},{"h":"Pachisi’s look-alike","p":["Pieces circling a cross-shaped board, moves set by how many thrown lots land face up: patolli and India’s pachisi and chaupar are astonishingly alike. In 1878 the British anthropologist Tylor argued that this was no coincidence but a sign that the game had come from Asia.","Yet no evidence has been found of such a crossing of the oceans. Today most think that people in different places arrived separately at the same ideas: a road that goes round, and lots to throw."],"cap":["A painting of chaupar in India (1694–95). Pieces travelling round a cross-shaped board, just as in patolli"]}],"tl":[["Teotihuacan era","Cross-shaped boards are cut into stone floors"],["1519–21","Spain conquers the Aztecs"],["16th c.","The Codex Magliabechiano shows patolli and Macuilxochitl"],["c. 1577","Sahagún records patolli in the Florentine Codex"],["c. 1581","Durán describes the prayers and the gambling"],["1878","Tylor argues for an Asian origin"],["1977","A board is found among rock art in Jalisco"]]},"xiangqi":{"title":"The river and the cannon of xiangqi","lead":"A “river” across the middle and a cannon that jumps to capture: when did Chinese chess take its present shape?","s":["Xiangqi is Chinese chess, played on the points of a 9×10 grid. Its pieces are round discs with characters on them, and they move on the lines, not inside squares as in chess.","An early clue is the Xuanguai lu, a book of strange tales by the Tang minister Niu Sengru (779–847). A man’s dream of a tiny army names a heavenly horse, a general, chariots and foot soldiers, with hints of a catapult. Its board and moves, though, are not today’s.","The present form — the 9×10 board, the river and the cannon — appears in records of the Song dynasty (960–1279). A full set of pieces has been found in a Northern Song tomb at Luoyang.","The river is often labelled “Chu River, Han Border”, after the line between Xiang Yu’s Chu and Liu Bang’s Han in their war of the 3rd century BC. The general cannot leave his palace, and the two generals may never face each other with nothing between them.","The cannon captures only by jumping over exactly one piece. Xiangqi is loved in Vietnam as cờ tướng, and in Korea it grew into the close cousin janggi. In the Atlas you can play both."],"quiz":[["Where do xiangqi pieces move?","On the points of the lines","Inside squares","Around a circle","On hexagons"],["In which dynasty does today’s xiangqi appear in records?","Song","Han","Ming","Qing"],["How does the cannon capture?","By jumping over one piece","One step straight ahead","Two steps diagonally","Only after crossing the river"]],"kf":["Chinese chess: round, lettered pieces fight on the points of a 9 × 10 grid of lines","A “river” crosses the middle and each side has a “palace” for its general; each piece has its own ground","It took its present form in the Song dynasty and is still played by tens of millions in China and Vietnam"],"more":[{"h":"A board with a river and palaces","p":["Each side has 16 pieces: a general, 2 advisors, 2 elephants, 2 horses, 2 chariots, 2 cannons and 5 soldiers. Red and Black write some of the same roles with slightly different characters.","The general and advisors may not leave the palace, and elephants may not cross the river. Soldiers only move forward until they cross the river, after which they may also step sideways. A horse cannot leap if a piece stands right next to it in the direction it wants to go. The board itself shapes what each piece can do."],"cap":["Xiangqi’s starting position. Black above, Red below. The words 楚河 漢界 mark the river; the 3 × 3 areas with diagonals are the palaces"]},{"h":"The leaping cannon","p":["A cannon normally moves like a chariot, any distance along a line. But to capture, it must jump over exactly one piece in between — of either colour — called the screen.","Using pieces as screens creates tactics found nowhere else. Moving a cannon to the centre file at the start, the “central cannon”, is still one of the most popular openings."],"cap":["How the cannon captures: the red cannon leaps exactly one piece in between — the black horse — to take the black chariot beyond"]},{"h":"Game records and masters","p":["In the Ming dynasty many books collected fine games and problems. Shiqing yaqu (1570) and Juzhong mi (1632) are classics still studied today.","In the 20th century Hu Ronghua won China’s national championship ten times in a row, and in 1993 the World Xiangqi Federation was founded, bringing international tournaments. In parks in China and Vietnam, circles of onlookers still gather round the board every day."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["9th c.","Niu Sengru’s Xuanguai lu mentions generals, chariots and horses"],["Song (960–1279)","The present form, with 9 × 10 board, river and cannon, appears in records"],["Northern Song","A full set of pieces is placed in a tomb in Luoyang"],["1570","The game collection Shiqing yaqu"],["1632","The game collection Juzhong mi"],["1960s–70s","Hu Ronghua wins ten national titles in a row"],["1993","The World Xiangqi Federation is founded"]]},"backgammon":{"title":"Is backgammon 5,000 years old?","lead":"The board called “the oldest backgammon” and the game’s real ancestors — from a Persian court to the clubs of New York.","s":["The “Burnt City” of Shahr-e Sukhteh in Iran yielded a board about 5,000 years old, with 60 pieces and dice, and it is often called the oldest backgammon. But its fields are formed by the coils of a carved snake, about twenty of them — a different shape from today’s game.","Backgammon’s likely direct ancestor is the Persian nard, which appeared between the 3rd and 6th centuries. A Middle Persian romance written between the 7th and 9th centuries says the minister Bozorgmehr, after solving the riddle of chess sent from India, invented nard in return.","Rome had its own board game played with three dice, tabula — Emperor Zeno’s unlucky game in story 3. This family of “tables” spread across Europe and produced many relatives, such as French trictrac.","The name “backgammon” appears in 17th-century England, and in 1743 Edmond Hoyle set out its rules and strategy in print.","The last great invention, the doubling cube, is said to have come from New York clubs in the 1920s. Able to double the stakes mid-game, it turned backgammon into a deep game of judging when to press."],"quiz":[["What shape is the Shahr-e Sukhteh board?","Fields made by a snake’s coils","The same as backgammon today","A round disc","A cross"],["Who, in the romance, invented nard?","The minister Bozorgmehr","Emperor Zeno","Edmond Hoyle","Alfonso X"],["What is said to have come from 1920s New York?","The doubling cube","Two dice","The 24 points","Fifteen pieces"]],"kf":["Fifteen pieces each race over 24 triangular points by the roll of two dice; the first to bear them all off wins","Its direct ancestors are the Persian nard and the Roman tabula and other “tables” games","The doubling cube and computers gave it new depth in the 20th century"],"more":[{"h":"Fifteen checkers and 24 points","p":["The board has 24 long triangles, or points, and each player has 15 checkers. You roll two dice and move one checker by each number, or one checker by both. A double lets you use the number four times.","Landing on a lone enemy checker hits it and sends it to the bar in the middle; it must re-enter through its opponent’s home board. Once all fifteen of your checkers are in your home board you start bearing them off, and the first to clear the board wins."],"cap":["The backgammon starting position. White moves from 24 down to 1 (anticlockwise); the bottom-right points 1–6 are its home board. Black moves the opposite way"]},{"h":"Closer means easier to hit","p":["Whether you can hit a lone checker depends greatly on distance. Six points away, 17 of the 36 rolls hit; seven away, only 6.","Knowing these numbers tells you where a checker is in danger. At the start, each player needs a total of 167 pips to bring every checker home and off; strong players count this “pip count” to judge who is ahead."],"cap":["How many of the 36 rolls of two dice can hit a lone checker 1 to 12 points away (with nothing in the way)"]},{"h":"From clubs to world championships","p":["In 1743 Edmond Hoyle in England published a book of rules and strategy. In 19th-century Britain the game was loved enough to be painted on furniture, and in the 1920s the doubling cube was born in New York clubs.","A world championship was held in the Bahamas in 1964, and the game boomed in the 1970s. In 1979 Hans Berliner’s program beat world champion Luigi Villa, and around 1992 TD-Gammon, which learned by playing itself, reached a strength close to the best human players."],"cap":["A cabinet painted with two backgammon players (London, 1861), an early piece by Morris, Marshall, Faulkner & Co."]}],"tl":[["c. 3000 BC","A coiled-snake board, pieces and dice are buried at Shahr-e Sukhteh"],["c. 3rd–6th c.","Nard appears in Persia"],["1743","Hoyle publishes his book on backgammon"],["1920s","The doubling cube is born in New York"],["1964","A world championship is held in the Bahamas"],["1979","Berliner’s program beats the world champion"],["c. 1992","TD-Gammon, which learns by self-play, appears"]]},"hex":{"title":"Hex, invented twice","lead":"A Danish poet and a young American genius came up with the same game, each without knowing of the other.","s":["In Hex, players take turns placing stones on a rhombus of hexagons; whoever joins their own two opposite sides wins. That is the whole game.","The first to think of it was the Danish mathematician and poet Piet Hein. In 1942 he showed it at the Niels Bohr Institute in Copenhagen; on 26 December that year the newspaper Politiken printed it as “Polygon”, and it was sold as pads of 50 sheets.","In 1943 Hein had to leave Denmark, and the game faded away. A few years later, in 1948–49, John Nash, a graduate student at Princeton, invented the same game quite independently; his fellow players are said to have called it “Nash”.","In 1952 Parker Brothers sold it as “Hex”, and in 1957 Martin Gardner featured it in his Scientific American column, making it known around the world.","Hex can never end in a draw. And Nash proved that the first player always has a winning strategy — without showing what it is. On large boards that winning way is still unknown."],"quiz":[["How do you win at Hex?","Join your two sides","Capture every enemy stone","Surround the king","Make five in a row"],["What was Hein’s game called in the Danish newspaper?","Polygon","Nash","Con-tac-tix","Othello"],["What did Nash prove?","The first player can always win","The second player always wins","It is always a draw","The exact winning moves"]],"kf":["Take turns placing stones on hexagonal cells; join your two opposite edges to win","Invented separately by Piet Hein in Denmark in 1942 and John Nash in the US in 1948–49","There are no draws and the first player is proved to win — yet on large boards no one knows how"],"more":[{"h":"A game of joining","p":["The board is a rhombus of hexagonal cells; the standard size is 11 × 11. Red owns the top and bottom edges, Blue the left and right, and they take turns placing one stone at a time. Stones never move and are never captured.","When the board is full, one side is always connected. Hexagons never meet only at a corner, so you cannot slip past an opponent’s line. In 1979 the mathematician David Gale showed that this “no draws” property is equivalent to a famous result in mathematics, the Brouwer fixed-point theorem."],"cap":["A 7 × 7 hex board. Red wins by joining top and bottom, Blue by joining left and right. The gold-rimmed red stones have joined top to bottom"]},{"h":"Bridges and ladders","p":["A favourite tool of strong players is the bridge. Place a stone not next to your last but one cell away, and the two share two empty neighbours. If the opponent blocks one, you take the other, so in effect they are already joined.","Near the edges, many fixed patterns are known, such as “ladders” of stones running along to reach the side. Because the first player has too great an advantage, the swap rule is normally used: after the first move, the second player may take that stone as their own."],"cap":["The “bridge”. The two red stones can be joined through either of the two empty cells between them (dashed circles). If Blue takes one, Red takes the other"]},{"h":"A mathematician’s game","p":["Nash proved the first player wins with a “strategy-stealing” argument: if the second player had a winning strategy, the first could take it over, which is a contradiction. It is a curious proof that shows no actual winning moves.","In the 1950s Claude Shannon, the father of information theory, built a machine that played hex with an electrical circuit. In the 2010s computers worked out every winning first move on boards up to 9 × 9, but the standard 11 × 11 board is still unsolved."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1942","Hein publishes “Polygon” in a Danish newspaper"],["1948–49","Nash at Princeton comes up with the same game"],["1952","Parker Brothers sells it as “Hex”"],["1950s","Shannon builds an electrical hex-playing machine"],["1957","Gardner presents it in his magazine column"],["1979","Gale shows its link to the fixed-point theorem"],["2010s","Boards up to 9 × 9 are solved by computer"]]},"foxgeese":{"title":"The fox, the geese and the king’s silver pieces","lead":"One fox, many geese: the long history of a “hunt game” where the two sides are not equal at all.","s":["In fox and geese, a single fox jumps over geese to eat them, while the geese try to pen the fox in so it cannot move. The two sides play by different rules on a cross-shaped board of 33 points joined by lines.","An early clue is “halatafl” in the Icelandic Grettis saga, written around the 14th century. It means “tail board”, the tail presumably being a fox’s. It was probably an offshoot of the Norse tafl games.","In England, the 15th-century accounts of King Edward IV record the purchase of “two foxes and 26 hounds of silver overgilt” — pieces for two sets, apparently called “fox and hounds” then.","With the original 13 geese the fox was too strong, so ways of playing with more geese spread. In the 19th century Queen Victoria is said to have been fond of the game.","The same cross-shaped board serves for the one-player peg solitaire. Hunt games where a tiger or wolf chases its prey, like Nepal’s bagh-chal, are found all over the world. In the Atlas you can play both fox and geese and peg solitaire."],"quiz":[["What does the “hala” in halatafl mean?","Tail","Fox","King","Snow"],["What pieces are in Edward IV’s accounts?","Silver foxes and hounds","Golden geese","An ivory king","Wooden dice"],["Which one-player game uses the same cross-shaped board?","Peg solitaire","Sudoku","Solitaire (cards)","Tangram"]],"kf":["One fox against 13 (later 17) geese on a cross-shaped board of 33 points","The fox captures by jumping; the geese win by hemming the fox in so it cannot move","The same board serves for peg solitaire, and hunt games of this kind are found worldwide"],"more":[{"h":"One against thirteen","p":["The fox moves one step along a line and captures by jumping a neighbouring goose if the point beyond is empty; as in draughts, it may jump several in a row. The geese cannot capture, and in many versions they cannot move backwards.","The geese aim to close ranks without gaps, corner the fox and leave it no move. With thirteen geese the fox is said to have too much of an advantage; with seventeen geese that may not step backwards, good play is said to let the geese win."],"cap":["A fox and geese board: 33 points on a cross, joined by lines. Thirteen geese stand at the top; the fox (orange) can take the goose just ahead (×) by jumping it to the empty point beyond"]},{"h":"The family of hunt games","p":["A few strong pieces chasing many weak ones: such hunt games are found all over the world. In Nepal’s bagh-chal, four tigers hunt twenty goats on a 5 × 5 board; in 2004 computation showed it to be a draw with perfect play.","Asalto, popular in 19th-century Germany, uses the same cross-shaped board: two officers against twenty-four soldiers. Here the officers defend a fortress that the soldiers try to storm."],"cap":["Hunt games by numbers: hunters on the left (orange), hunted on the right (white). From the top: fox and geese (1:13), the version with more geese (1:17), bagh-chal from Nepal (4:20), and asalto (2:24)"]},{"h":"From royal pieces to peg solitaire","p":["The accounts of Edward IV of England (reigned 1461–83) record the purchase of gilded silver pieces for a fox and hounds. It was a pastime of kings and nobles too.","The same cross-shaped board of 33 holes is used for peg solitaire, played alone. A French engraving of 1697 shows a noblewoman playing it — one of the oldest records of peg solitaire."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["c. 14th c.","“Hala-tafl” appears in the Icelandic Grettis saga"],["late 15th c.","Edward IV buys silver pieces for fox and hounds"],["1697","A French engraving shows a noblewoman playing peg solitaire"],["19th c.","Queen Victoria is said to have enjoyed the game"],["19th c.","Asalto, on the same board, becomes popular in Germany"],["2004","Bagh-chal from Nepal is shown to be a draw with perfect play"]]},"latrunculi":{"title":"The Roman soldiers’ game","lead":"A game the Romans loved, named after hired soldiers. No rulebook survives — only poems, anecdotes and boards found in the ground.","s":["Ludus latrunculorum was a two-player battle game of ancient Rome. Latrunculi originally meant hired soldiers or bandits, and the pieces stood for soldiers.","Pieces moved on a grid, and a piece caught between two enemy pieces was taken. A 1st-century poem, the Laus Pisonis, describes pieces advancing and falling back, and a piece hemmed in until it cannot move.","The philosopher Seneca tells of Julius Canus, condemned to death by the emperor Caligula. When the centurion came for him, Canus was playing latrunculi; he counted the pieces and told his opponent not to claim after his death that he had won.","Boards have been found all over the empire. Grids cut into the stones of forts and towns range from about 8 × 8 to 12 × 12. A 1st-century grave at Stanway in England held glass pieces still laid out on their board.","No detailed rulebook survives, and today’s rules are reconstructions by researchers. The Atlas’s latrunculi is based on one of them."],"quiz":[["How is a piece captured in latrunculi?","By trapping it between two pieces","By jumping over it","By a roll of the dice","By surrounding the king"],["Who was playing latrunculi when the escort came to take him to execution?","Julius Canus","Caesar","Nero","Cicero"],["What was still lying on the board in the Stanway grave?","Glass pieces","Gold coins","Chessmen","Playing cards"]],"kf":["A two-player battle game of ancient Rome, its pieces seen as soldiers","A piece trapped between two enemies is taken; pieces that cannot move are mentioned too","No rulebook survives: the game is reconstructed from poems, anecdotes and excavated boards"],"more":[{"h":"Trap and surround","p":["The fullest source for reconstructions is the 1st-century poem Laus Pisonis. It describes the game like a battlefield: a piece moves forward to tempt the enemy, another guards its back, and a piece caught between two enemies is lost.","The poem also speaks of a piece hemmed in and unable to move, so many reconstructions add a rule for blocked pieces besides capture by trapping. The Nordic tafl games use a very similar trap between two pieces."],"cap":["Capturing in latrunculi (as reconstructed). Left: moving as the blue arrow shows traps the white piece between two blacks, and it is taken (×). Right: a white piece blocked on all four sides cannot move"]},{"h":"A board with two faces","p":["In a poem written for a gift, the poet Martial (1st century) describes a board on which pieces move by the throw of the dice on one side, while soldiers of two colours fight on the other — a game of luck and a game of skill on a single board.","The dice side is thought to be the race game called “twelve lines”, a forerunner of tabula and backgammon. The dice tower (pyrgus) in the museum’s collection is another tool the Romans used."],"cap":["Martial’s two-sided board, as imagined: a race game of twelve lines played with dice on one face, the soldiers’ grid on the other"]},{"h":"Boards from the ground","p":["Roman soldiers cut grids into the stones and floors of their forts to play. Incised boards have been found near Hadrian’s Wall in Britain, and in Germany, Spain and North Africa.","In the Stanway grave (mid-1st century) the wooden board had rotted, but the white and blue glass pieces on it were still in place, as if mid-game. Their owner may have been a doctor: medical instruments were buried with them."],"cap":["A dice tower (pyrgus). Romans threw dice through towers like this to stop cheating"]}],"tl":[["1st c. BC","Varro mentions latrunculi"],["late 1st c. BC","Ovid mentions the game in his love poetry"],["1st c.","The Laus Pisonis describes the game in verse"],["mid-1st c.","A board laid out with pieces is buried at Stanway"],["1st c.","Seneca tells the story of Julius Canus"],["late 1st c.","Martial writes of a two-sided board"],["1994","Schädler publishes a reconstruction of the rules"]]},"amazons":{"title":"The game that burns its own board","lead":"Born in Argentina in 1988, the Game of the Amazons joins the moves of a chess queen to territory counting as in go.","s":["The Game of the Amazons is a two-player game on a 10 × 10 board, each side with four “amazons”. It was invented in 1988 by Walter Zamkauskas of Argentina.","A move has two parts. First an amazon moves like a chess queen, any distance in a straight line across, up and down or diagonally; then, from where it lands, it shoots an arrow the same way. The square the arrow lands on can never be crossed again.","Each move fills one more square, and the player who can no longer move loses. There are no draws.","Late in the game the arrows wall the board into separate rooms. Then the player with more empty squares of their own wins, so the position is judged by counting territory, as in go.","There are more than 2,000 possible first moves, which makes the game hard even for computers. Mathematicians study it, and it is an event at computer tournaments."],"quiz":[["In which country was the Game of the Amazons born?","Argentina","Brazil","Spain","Mexico"],["What happens to a square an arrow is shot into?","No one can cross it again","It disappears next turn","It becomes the opponent’s piece","It scores a point"],["Who loses in the Game of the Amazons?","The first player unable to move","The first to lose a piece","The first to make a territory","The one who runs out of time"]],"kf":["A two-player game on a 10 × 10 board, born in Argentina in 1988","An amazon moves like a queen, then shoots an arrow from where it lands to burn a square","The player who cannot move loses; at the end, territory is counted as in go"],"more":[{"h":"Move, then shoot","p":["An amazon may move any number of squares, as long as it does not pass another piece or an arrow. From the square it reaches, it then shoots an arrow, again with a queen’s move — even back into the square it has just left.","Nothing is ever captured. Instead, each move closes one square of the board and blocks the opponent’s paths: attack and defence are decided by the same single arrow."],"cap":["A move in the Game of the Amazons. The white amazon moves like a queen (blue), then shoots an arrow the same way from where it lands (red). The arrow’s square is closed for good"]},{"h":"A board split into rooms","p":["As the game goes on, the arrows wall the board into rooms. If only one player’s amazons are in a room, its empty squares belong to that player alone, to be used up one per move.","Then the game becomes a count: whoever has more usable squares can keep moving longer. This suits the mathematics of combinatorial game theory, and Elwyn Berlekamp and others studied Amazons endgames in detail."],"cap":["A late position (illustration). Walls of burnt squares split the board into four rooms: green rooms only White can use, blue rooms only Black. The side with more empty squares can keep moving longer"]},{"h":"Born in a magazine, played by computers","p":["The game is said to have first appeared in an Argentine puzzle magazine. Its rules are short and it needs only a chessboard and some pieces, so it spread worldwide through the internet.","With more than 2,000 possible first moves, the opening calls for a feel for the whole board, much as in go. Since the 2000s it has been an event at computer tournaments, where programs compete against each other."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1988","Walter Zamkauskas invents the Game of the Amazons"],["1990s","It spreads worldwide through the internet"],["2000","Berlekamp publishes mathematical work on its endgames"],["2000s","It becomes an event at the Computer Olympiad"]]},"loa":{"game":"Lines of Action","title":"Pieces that move as far as you count","lead":"Lines of Action came into the world in a 1969 book by Sid Sackson. You do not win by capturing: you win by joining all your pieces into one.","s":["Lines of Action (LOA) is a two-player game on an 8 × 8 board with twelve pieces a side. It was invented by Claude Soucie and introduced in 1969 in A Gamut of Games, a book by the game collector Sid Sackson.","A piece moves in a straight line, across, up and down or diagonally. How far it goes is exactly the number of pieces — yours and your opponent’s — on that line. Each move changes the counts, so the distances change too.","You may jump over your own pieces but never over your opponent’s. If you land on an opponent’s piece, you capture it.","The aim is to join all your pieces into a single group, touching across, up and down or diagonally. Capturing makes the opponent’s army smaller, which can even help it join up — a curious twist.","The rules are short but the game is hard to read ahead, so it has long been used in game-AI research. Programs from Maastricht University in the Netherlands have won computer tournaments many times."],"quiz":[["What decides how many squares a piece moves?","The number of pieces on that line","The roll of a die","The number of pieces captured so far","It is always three squares"],["How do you win at LOA?","By joining all your pieces into one group","By capturing all the opponent’s pieces","By reaching the far edge of the board","By capturing the opponent’s king"],["Who introduced LOA in a 1969 book?","Sid Sackson","Martin Gardner","Edmond Hoyle","H. J. R. Murray"]],"kf":["A two-player game on an 8 × 8 board, twelve pieces each; invented by Claude Soucie and introduced in Sid Sackson’s 1969 book","A piece moves exactly as many squares as there are pieces on its line; it may jump its own pieces, never the enemy’s","The winner is not who captures most but who first joins all their pieces into one group"],"more":[{"h":"Count, then move","p":["If, say, three pieces stand on the rank through a piece, that piece moves exactly three squares along it — no more, no less. It may pass over its own pieces on the way, but if an opponent’s piece is in between, it cannot go that way.","Each piece sits on four lines — a rank, a file and two diagonals — and each has its own count. When one piece moves, the counts on several lines around it change, so the “distances” on the board are reshuffled every move. That is what makes LOA so hard to see through."],"cap":["Where the black piece where the arrows start can go. The rank holds 3 pieces, so it moves 3 (jumping its own piece is fine); the file holds 2, so it moves 2 and captures a white piece. The diagonal also holds 3, but a white piece is in the way (×)"]},{"h":"One group wins","p":["Pieces count as joined when they touch across, up and down or diagonally. A player left with a single piece is “joined” too, so capturing too much can even hand your opponent the win.","What happens when one move joins both sides at once differs by edition and tournament. The first edition of Sackson’s book called it a draw, a later one a win for the player who moved, and most tournaments today score it as a draw. In this Atlas’s LOA the player who moved wins."],"cap":["A winning position. The white pieces (gold rims) touch one another, diagonals included, and form one group. Black is still split into three (dotted)"]},{"h":"A game for computers to fight over","p":["With a small board, short rules and deep play, LOA became a test bed for game AI. MIA, the program written by Mark Winands at Maastricht University, won the Computer Olympiad in 2003, 2004, 2006 and 2009, and his work was gathered in his 2004 PhD thesis, Informed Search in Complex Games.","In 2008 Winands solved LOA on a smaller 6 × 6 board, showing that with perfect play the first player (Black) wins. The full 8 × 8 game is believed to be still unsolved. Human players meet too, at a world championship held every year at the Mind Sports Olympiad."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["c. 1960","Claude Soucie is said to have invented LOA"],["1969","Introduced in Sid Sackson’s A Gamut of Games"],["2003","Winands’s MIA wins the Computer Olympiad (and again in 2004, 2006 and 2009)"],["2004","Winands’s PhD thesis, Informed Search in Complex Games"],["2008","6 × 6 LOA is solved: a win for the first player"]]},"konane":{"title":"The game of black lava and white coral","lead":"Kōnane, played by the people of Hawaiʻi, was written down in the account of Cook’s voyage — and in the 20th century mathematicians took it apart with combinatorial game theory.","s":["Kōnane is a two-player game played by Native Hawaiians. Black and white stones fill the board in a checkered pattern with no gaps, and players capture by jumping over each other’s stones.","In the old days the black stones were lava and the white ones pieces of coral. The board, called a papamū, was a large flat stone with hollows pecked into its surface. The game is said to appear even in the Kumulipo, the Hawaiian creation chant.","First Black removes one of its own stones, then White removes a neighbouring one. The two holes this leaves are where the jumping begins.","Every move is a capture. A stone jumps up, down or sideways over an adjacent enemy stone and lands on the empty square beyond. As long as it goes straight, it may keep jumping.","The player who has no capture left loses; there are no draws. This simple design later caught the interest of mathematicians."],"quiz":[["What were the old white stones of kōnane made of?","Coral","Ivory","White marble","Animal bone"],["Who loses at kōnane?","The player with no capture left","The player whose stones are all taken","The first to reach the edge of the board","The player left with a single stone"],["When making several jumps, what is not allowed?","Changing direction on the way","Taking more than one stone in a turn","Stopping partway","Landing next to one of your own stones"]],"kf":["A two-player game of the Native Hawaiians, begun with black (lava) and white (coral) stones filling the board alternately","Every move jumps an enemy stone up, down or sideways and captures it; going straight, a stone may jump again","The player with no capture left loses; mathematicians studied the game with combinatorial game theory"],"more":[{"h":"Jump and capture","p":["Jumps go only up, down or sideways, never diagonally. If the square beyond an adjacent enemy stone is empty, the stone jumps over it, lands, and the jumped stone is taken off the board.","If it can jump again from where it lands, it may carry on in the same direction. It may not change direction on the way, and it may stop whenever it likes. Only one stone moves in a turn."],"cap":["A white stone jumps twice to the right in one turn, taking two black stones (blue 1 and 2). It may not turn and jump downward from where it lands (×)"]},{"h":"The first two holes","p":["On a board packed with stones, nobody can jump. So Black, who starts, lifts one of its own stones from the centre four squares or from a corner. White then lifts one of its stones that touches that hole up, down or sideways.","The two holes side by side are the first landing places. Where they are opened shapes the whole game that follows."],"cap":["The two ways to begin. Black removes one of its stones from the centre four (left) or a corner (right) (1), then White removes a neighbouring stone (2)"]},{"h":"Stone boards and 238 squares","p":["The account of James Cook’s third voyage (volume III, published in 1784) describes a stone game seen in Hawaiʻi as “very much like our draughts”, but more intricate. It was written by the officer James King, who says the board was about two feet long, with 238 squares, fourteen in a row.","There was no fixed size: boards of 6 × 7, 9 × 13, 13 × 20 and more are known. Today the game is usually played on an even, square board, and this Atlas offers 6 × 6, 8 × 8 and 10 × 10. Papamū carved in stone can still be seen at Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park on the Island of Hawaiʻi."],"cap":["Board sizes compared: the 14 × 17 board (238 squares) described in the account of Cook’s voyage, and the 10 × 10, 8 × 8 and 6 × 6 boards you can play in this Atlas"]},{"h":"A game the mathematicians found","p":["Kōnane is lost by the player who cannot move, and late in the game the board breaks into independent parts — a perfect fit for the combinatorial game theory of Elwyn Berlekamp and his colleagues. Michael Ernst wrote a short paper on kōnane with Berlekamp in 1993, and in a 1995 paper worked out the values of many positions.","More followed: Alice Chan and Alice Tsai studied one-row kōnane (2002), and Bob Hearn showed that on ever larger boards, deciding who wins becomes extremely hard (PSPACE-complete). A game of jumping stones had become a subject for the theory of computation."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1778","Cook’s ships reach Hawaiʻi"],["1784","The account of Cook’s third voyage is published, with King’s description of the stone game"],["1993","Ernst and Berlekamp write a short paper on kōnane"],["1995","Ernst’s paper “Playing Konane Mathematically”"],["2002","Chan and Tsai sum up their results on one-row kōnane"]]},"peg":{"title":"The jumping puzzle born at a royal court","lead":"Peg solitaire was played at the French court of Louis XIV in the late 17th century. A simple puzzle of taking pegs away one by one, it has fascinated mathematicians ever since.","s":["Peg solitaire is a puzzle for one player. Pegs fill the holes of a board, with just one hole left empty.","A peg jumps over a neighbouring peg, up, down or sideways, into the empty hole beyond. The peg jumped over is taken off the board.","The goal is to keep jumping until a single peg is left. Leaving it in the very centre is the perfect finish.","The first firm records come from late 17th-century France, at the court of Louis XIV. In 1697 the magazine Mercure galant printed the board and its rules, and an engraving shows a noblewoman with a board beside her.","It looks easy but is hard. The mathematician Leibniz took an interest, and in the 20th century mathematicians explained, by colouring the holes, why some problems can never be solved."],"quiz":[["What happens to a peg that is jumped over?","It is taken off the board","It becomes the opponent’s","It slides into the next hole","It comes back next turn"],["How many holes does the English board have?","33","37","45","64"],["Where do the first firm records of peg solitaire come from?","The French court of Louis XIV, late 17th century","The baths of ancient Rome","A temple school in Edo-period Japan","A saloon in 19th-century America"]],"kf":["A jumping puzzle for one: jump pegs to take them, and leave a single peg to win","Firm records begin around 1697 at the French court of Louis XIV; the classic boards have 33 (English) and 37 (European) holes","Colouring the holes in three colours tells you in advance which problems cannot be solved"],"more":[{"h":"Jump and take","p":["Jumps go only up, down or sideways, never diagonally (unless the board has diagonal lines, like the triangle). Every jump removes one peg, so starting from 32 pegs it takes 31 jumps to get down to one.","The same peg may jump several times in a row. Counting such a chain as one move, the English board’s centre-to-centre problem can be solved in 18 moves. Ernest Bergholt found this solution in 1912, and in 1964 John Beasley proved that it cannot be done in fewer."],"cap":["The first move on the English board. With the centre empty, the peg at the top jumps over the one below it into the centre (blue); the jumped peg is taken (×)"]},{"h":"From a royal pastime","p":["The firm history of peg solitaire goes back to the court of Louis XIV around 1697. That August the magazine Mercure galant described the board, the rules and some problems, and the engraver Claude-Auguste Berey portrayed the Princesse de Soubise with a board at her side.","There is also a well-known story that a nobleman locked up in the Bastille invented the puzzle to pass the time, but it is a legend that cannot be confirmed. The 37-hole board used in France is said to date from the 17th century too, while the 33-hole board with its corners cut off is called the English board."],"cap":["The two classic boards: English, 33 holes (left), and European, 37 holes (right). The empty hole is each board’s usual starting hole"]},{"h":"What three colours reveal","p":["Colour the holes in three colours so that the colours run along the diagonals. Then any three holes in a row, across or up and down, are always three different colours. A jump takes one peg away from two colours and adds one to the third, so the counts of all three colours switch between odd and even together.","On the European board with the centre empty, each colour has 12 pegs — all even. Each jump only switches between “all even” and “all odd”, so the position of a lone last peg — one colour with 1, the other two with 0 — can never be reached. On this board, the problem starting from the centre has no solution. The same check on the English board narrows the holes where a single last peg can end, starting from the centre, to just five."],"cap":["The European 37-hole board with its holes coloured in three colours along the diagonals. With the centre empty, each colour has 12 pegs"]},{"h":"A plaything for mathematicians","p":["Leibniz is reported to have written, in a note of 1710, that he liked to play the game backwards — filling in the holes that had been jumped. Working back from the end is still a basic way to solve it.","Winning Ways (1982), by Berlekamp, Conway and Guy, analysed the puzzle closely and introduced “pagoda functions”: give each hole a score so that no jump can raise the total, and you can prove that some goals are impossible. In 1999, a computer search is said to have examined every position."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1697","Played at the court of Louis XIV; the August Mercure galant prints the board and rules"],["1710","Leibniz is said to write about playing the game backwards"],["1912","Bergholt finds an 18-move solution on the English board"],["1964","Beasley proves that no solution is shorter than 18 moves"],["1982","Winning Ways sets out its analysis, including pagoda functions"]]},"fanorona":{"title":"Capture by approach, capture by withdrawal","lead":"Fanorona is Madagascar’s national game. Legend says it once decided a throne — and in the 21st century computers showed that with best play it is a draw.","s":["Fanorona is a two-player game widely played in Madagascar. It uses a 9 × 5 board drawn with lines across, up and down and diagonally, and 22 pieces a side.","The board looks like two alquerque boards — a game of the Arab world and Europe — set side by side. Alquerque is thought to have reached the island and grown there into a game of its own.","Its great feature is how pieces are taken. Moving towards an enemy piece (“approach”) or away from one (“withdrawal”) captures the whole row of enemy pieces on that line.","Capturing is compulsory, and a piece that has captured may change direction and capture again. A single turn can change the board completely — the thrill of fanorona.","Loved for centuries, it even has a legend of a prince who lost a throne because he could not stop a game. In 2007 Dutch researchers used computers to show that with best play on both sides the game is a draw."],"quiz":[["What size is the fanorona board?","9 × 5","8 × 8","7 × 7","6 × 6"],["What are fanorona’s two ways of capturing?","Approach and withdrawal","Jumping and sandwiching","Surrounding and bumping","Dice and lots"],["What did computers show happens with best play?","A draw","A win for the first player","A win for the second player","Nothing is known yet"]],"kf":["A two-player game of Madagascar: 22 pieces each on a 9 × 5 board, with only the centre empty at the start","Moving towards (approach) or away from (withdrawal) an enemy piece captures the whole row on that line","In 2007 computers showed that with best play the game is a draw"],"more":[{"h":"Approach and withdrawal","p":["In an approach, if the point just beyond where the piece lands holds an enemy piece, that piece and every enemy piece lined up behind it without a gap are taken. Withdrawal is the reverse: the enemy piece right behind the starting point, and those lined up behind it, are taken.","Since one step can remove several pieces at once, the armies shrink fast from the very start. When a move allows both an approach and a withdrawal, the player chooses one."],"cap":["The two captures. Top: White steps right towards a black piece and takes the three blacks in a row beyond (approach). Bottom: White steps right, away from a black piece, and takes the two blacks behind it (withdrawal)"]},{"h":"Two alquerque boards side by side","p":["The board has 5 × 9 = 45 points; besides the lines across and up and down, diagonals run through every other point. Pieces move one step along a line to a neighbouring point. At the start only the centre point is empty, so the first move is always a capture.","The board is two 5 × 5 alquerque boards joined together. Alquerque is an old game already named in a 10th-century Arabic book, and fanorona is thought to have grown from it. When and how it reached the island is unclear; some books say around the 17th century."],"cap":["The starting position: 22 black and 22 white pieces on the 9 × 5 points, with only the centre point empty. The dashed squares mark two 5 × 5 alquerque boards"]},{"h":"Chains of captures","p":["A piece that has captured may capture again. But each step must go in a different direction, and it may not return to a point it has already visited. Whether to go on is up to the player.","When such a chain works, one turn can take almost half the enemy’s pieces. If the chain ends in a bad spot, though, the piece is quickly taken back."],"cap":["A capturing chain. White (1) approaches to the right, taking two; (2) withdraws upward, taking two; (3) approaches to the left, taking two. After (1) it may not go on to the right (×)"]},{"h":"A throne and the computers","p":["Legend says that Ralambo, a king of Imerina around the 16th century, pretended to be ill so that the first son to come home would inherit the throne. The elder, Andriantompokoindrindra, could not tear himself away from a hard fanorona position, and the younger, Andrianjaka, became king. Another story says that during the French invasion of 1895 the queen’s side relied on a ritual game of fanorona; neither tale can be confirmed.","In 2007 Maarten Schadd and colleagues at Maastricht University in the Netherlands worked out every position with seven pieces or fewer and used proof-number search to show that the starting position is a draw (the paper appeared in 2008). The computation took more than a week on a single machine."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["c. 16th c.","The legend of King Ralambo’s succession and fanorona"],["c. 17th c.","One view of when fanorona grew from alquerque"],["1895","The legend of a ritual fanorona game during the French invasion"],["2007","Schadd and colleagues show by computer that it is a draw"],["2008","The paper “Best Play in Fanorona Leads to Draw”"]]},"janggi":{"title":"A board without a river, and a cannon that leaps just once","lead":"Janggi, beloved in Korea, uses the same 9 × 10 board as Chinese xiangqi — but it removed the river and changed how the pieces move.","s":["Janggi is a two-player chess game long played in Korea. It is thought to come from Chinese xiangqi, and its pieces stand on the points of a 9 × 10 grid of lines.","Unlike xiangqi, the board has no “river” across the middle, so elephants and soldiers can go anywhere. The general (king) starts in the middle of its “palace” and may never leave it.","The sides are Cho (blue or green) and Han (red). The pieces are octagonal, and the stronger the piece, the larger it is made. Cho moves first.","Before play, each side chooses one of four ways to place its horses and elephants. Han sets up first and Cho decides after seeing it, so the bargaining begins before the first move.","Besides checkmate, games can be decided on points. Cho, who moves first, starts with 72 points; Han gets a 1.5-point “deom” and starts with 73.5. Each piece lost lowers the score."],"quiz":[["What does the janggi board not have?","A river across the middle","Palaces","Diagonal lines in the palaces","90 points"],["What must a cannon always do to move?","Jump over exactly one piece","Stand next to an enemy piece","Be inside a palace","Not be in check"],["When scoring, how many points of “deom” does Han, the second player, get?","1.5","0.5","3","6.5"]],"kf":["Korea’s chess, born from xiangqi: a 9 × 10 board, but without the river","The elephant moves one straight and two diagonal; the cannon moves and captures by jumping exactly one piece","Each side picks one of four horse-and-elephant setups; in scoring, Han gets a 1.5-point deom"],"more":[{"h":"A riverless board and four setups","p":["The janggi board has the same 9 × 10 points as xiangqi but no river in the middle. Each palace has diagonals in an X, and the general and guards move one step at a time inside it, along the diagonals too. The general starts in the very centre of its palace.","There are four setups, made by swapping horses and elephants. Han sets up first, and Cho chooses after seeing it. Whether the elephants stand inside or outside changes the lines of attack in the opening."],"cap":["The janggi board and the opening position. There is no river, and the palaces at top and bottom have diagonals. The dashed rings mark where horses and elephants can be swapped; Han (top) and Cho (bottom) have chosen different setups"]},{"h":"Horse, elephant and blocked points","p":["The horse first steps one point straight, then one point diagonally outward. The elephant goes further: one point straight, then two diagonally. For both, a piece on a point along the way blocks that direction.","The xiangqi elephant cannot cross the river and moves only two points diagonally, but the janggi elephant has long legs and can roam the whole board. In return, its path is easily blocked."],"cap":["The horse (left) moves one straight and one diagonal; the elephant (right) one straight and two diagonal. Blue dots show where they can go. A piece on a point along the way blocks that direction (×)"]},{"h":"The cannon that leaps just once","p":["The cannon must jump exactly one piece in between, both to move and to capture. With nothing in between it cannot move at all, so in the opening position the cannons are stuck.","Neither the piece jumped nor the piece captured may be a cannon, so two cannons can only glare at each other. Compared with the xiangqi cannon, which moves freely when not capturing, it is a much fussier piece."],"cap":["The cannon. Top: it jumps exactly one soldier and moves to a point beyond, or takes the chariot (blue). Middle: it cannot jump if the piece between is a cannon. Bottom: a cannon cannot take a cannon (×)"]},{"h":"Facing generals and points","p":["When the two generals face each other on a line with nothing between, it is called “bikjang”. By one convention, if the side faced does not break it, the game is a draw. Traditional rules also let a player pass a turn when not in check.","The values are chariot 13, cannon 7, horse 5, elephant 3, guard 3, soldier 2. Cho totals 72 points, and Han 73.5 with the deom. Because of the half point, a score can never be tied. In this Atlas’s janggi, these points decide the game when a position repeats three times or after 200 moves."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["Goryeo period","One view holds that it was already played (when it arrived is debated)"],["18th c.","A larger variant, gwangsanghui, is said to be recorded"],["1895","Stewart Culin’s Korean Games shows a picture of men playing janggi"],["2009","The first international tournament is said to be held in Harbin, China"]]},"pic":{"tlvmirror":"TLV mirror (Eastern Han, Cleveland Museum of Art)","kotoboard":"The Four Accomplishments (Edo period, c. 1620–50, Cleveland Museum of Art)","homerbg":"Winslow Homer, Backgammon (wood engraving, c. 1877, Cleveland Museum of Art)","trictrac":"Philips de Koninck, The Tric-Trac Players (drawing, c. 1660, Cleveland Museum of Art)","gametable":"Gaming table with chess and nine men’s morris boards (Mainz, c. 1735, Cleveland Museum of Art)","kota":"The ruler of Kota worshipping Krishna; a cross-shaped chaupar cloth lies on the steps (c. 1825, Cleveland Museum of Art)"},"mahjong":{"game":"Mahjong","title":"The tile game named after a sparrow","lead":"Mahjong was born in 19th-century China. A card game turned into tiles, swept America in the 1920s, and grew into riichi mahjong in Japan.","s":["Mahjong is a tile game for four players. It uses 136 tiles (144 with the flowers), and the first player to complete a hand of a set shape wins.","It was born in 19th-century China. Its roots are “draw and discard” games played with cards showing units of money, and the paper cards are thought to have turned into tiles around the middle of the century.","The oldest surviving tiles date from about 1870 and were acquired in Ningbo, Shanghai and Fuzhou. Some books say the game was born in Ningbo; others say it began as a pastime of fishermen in Fujian and Guangdong.","“Májiàng” means sparrow, and one theory traces the name to an older card game, madiao. The often-told story that Confucius invented it has no basis at all.","In the 20th century mahjong crossed the seas. It became a craze in 1920s America, and in Japan the later “riichi” rule helped it grow into a game of its own."],"quiz":[["Without the flowers, how many tiles does mahjong use?","136","144","108","152"],["What is the basic winning shape?","Four sets and one pair","Seven sets only","Thirteen identical tiles","Three sets and two pairs"],["In which country did mahjong become a craze in the 1920s?","The United States","Russia","Brazil","Egypt"]],"kf":["A four-player tile game born in 19th-century China from card games","Number tiles in characters, dots and bamboo, plus winds and dragons; you win with four sets and a pair","A craze in 1920s America; in post-war Japan it grew into riichi mahjong"],"more":[{"h":"136 tiles","p":["The number tiles come in three suits — characters, dots and bamboo — each numbered 1 to 9 with four copies: 108 tiles. Add four each of the east, south, west and north winds and the white, green and red dragons, and you have 136.","Many Chinese ways of playing also use eight flower tiles: plum, orchid, chrysanthemum and bamboo, and the four seasons. They are not part of the hand; when drawn they are set aside and add to the score. Japanese mahjong usually leaves them out."],"cap":["The kinds of tiles. Characters, dots and bamboo 1–9, the winds (east, south, west, north) and the dragons (white, green, red), four of each: 136. With the eight flowers below the dotted line (one of each): 144"]},{"h":"Four sets and a pair","p":["A hand has 13 tiles; players draw one and discard one, again and again. When a drawn tile, or a tile someone else throws away, completes four sets and a pair, the hand wins.","The sets are the chow (three numbers in a row in one suit), the pung (three identical tiles) and the kong (four identical tiles). Sets can also be made by claiming another player’s discard, so the game is a long contest of reading which tiles to take and which to let go."],"cap":["The basic winning shape: four sets — chows (three in a row of one suit), pungs (three of a kind) or kongs (four of a kind) — and one pair"]},{"h":"Mahjong crosses the seas","p":["In 1920 Joseph Park Babcock published a rule book in English, and a New York store began importing sets from China. Mahjong became a craze in 1920s America, and the National Mah Jongg League, founded in 1937, keeps an American way of playing alive.","It reached Japan in the 1920s; in 1924 Saburo Hirayama is said to have opened a parlour and school in Tokyo, and in 1929 the Japan Mahjong League was founded with the writer Kan Kikuchi as its head. In 1952, after the war, Daizō Amano is said to have published rules with “riichi” in a newspaper, and Japan’s own riichi mahjong spread from there."],"cap":["How mahjong spread: from China to America and Japan in the 1920s; riichi rules in Japan in 1952 and official Chinese rules in 1998"]},{"h":"Legends and real history","p":["Mahjong has many origin legends. The story that Confucius made it has no basis. The tale of a man in Ningbo named Chen Yumen who invented it is probably made up too, since the papers it is said to come from have never been found.","What is certain is that 19th-century Chinese draw-and-discard games played with money cards turned into a tile game. The early sets had no flower tiles and no green dragon, but “king” tiles and others instead. In 1998 China also laid down official national rules."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["mid-19th c.","Card games are thought to turn into a tile game"],["c. 1870","The oldest surviving sets (Ningbo, Shanghai, Fuzhou)"],["1920","Babcock publishes an English rule book"],["1929","The Japan Mahjong League is founded"],["1952","The “Hochi rules” with riichi are published"],["1998","China sets official national rules"]],"figL":{"hand":["Chow","Pung","Kong","Chow","Pair"],"spread":[["c. 1870","Ningbo, Shanghai","Oldest surviving sets"],["1920","United States","Babcock’s rules, a craze"],["1920s","Japan","Arrives; a league in 1929"],["1952","Japan","Riichi rules in a newspaper"],["1998","China","Official rules, 81 patterns"]]}},"gomoku":{"title":"Gomoku: when the first player was too strong","lead":"Gomoku, played with a go board and stones, has been loved since the Edo period. To curb the first player’s edge the rules of “renju” were born — and in the end computers found the answer.","s":["Gomoku is a two-player game played with a go board and stones. Black and White take turns placing stones, and the first to get five of their own in a row — across, up and down or diagonally — wins.","Its origins are unclear. Some say it goes back to ancient China or Heian Japan, but the firm records are from the Edo period, and it is said to have been played by the mid-18th century.","The more people played, the clearer it became that Black, moving first, had the advantage. So in Japan, rules were gradually made that placed “forbidden moves” on Black alone.","In 1899 Ruikō Kuroiwa of the newspaper Yorozu chōhō gave the game the name “renju”, meaning “connected pearls”.","In 1988 the Renju International Federation (RIF) was founded in Stockholm, Sweden, and world championships are held. Meanwhile, computers showed that gomoku without forbidden moves is a win for the first player."],"quiz":[["How do you win at gomoku?","Get five of your stones in a row","Surround and capture stones","Claim more than half the board","Capture five of the opponent’s stones"],["Who gave the game the name “renju”?","Ruikō Kuroiwa","Honinbō Dōsaku","Natsume Sōseki","Fukuzawa Yukichi"],["In renju, which moves are forbidden to Black only?","Double three, double four and overline","Playing on the edge of the board","Playing next to a white stone","Surrounding the opponent’s stones"]],"kf":["A game played with a go board and stones: five in a row wins; firm records begin in the Edo period","Because Black, moving first, has the edge, renju forbids Black the double three, double four and overline","In 1994 freestyle gomoku on 15 × 15 was proved a win for the first player"],"more":[{"h":"Five in a row, and the four-three","p":["Games are decided by a double threat the opponent cannot block in one move. A “four” — one stone short of five — must be blocked at once or the game is lost. An open “three”, free at both ends, becomes an open four if ignored, and then it cannot be stopped.","A move that makes a four and a three at the same time — a “four-three” — forces White to block the four, after which the three grows and wins. Getting better at gomoku means seeing this shape coming earlier and earlier."],"cap":["Left: five in a row diagonally wins. Right: the stone in the red ring makes a “four” across (play at 4 for five) and an open “three” down (the 3 points) at once — White cannot stop both (a four-three)"]},{"h":"Forbidden moves for Black only","p":["In renju Black may not play a double three, a double four or an overline (six or more in a row); doing so is a foul and loses. White has no forbidden moves, and even six or more in a row wins for White.","To win, Black must build shapes like the four-three that are not forbidden. White can even drive Black into a forbidden move on purpose — a battle of wits that gives renju its depth."],"cap":["Moves forbidden to Black in renju (×). From the left: a “double three” (two threes at once), a “double four” (two fours at once) and an “overline” (six or more)"]},{"h":"Gomoku solved","p":["In 1994 Victor Allis of the Netherlands proved by computer that in freestyle gomoku on a 15 × 15 board, the first player, Black, can always win. In 2001 Wágner and Virág showed that renju without opening rules is a first-player win too.","People still play because the winning lines are far too complex to memorise. Tournaments use “opening rules” that settle how the first few moves are made — such as Yamaguchi or Soosõrv-8 — to make the contest fair."],"cap":["The story of gomoku and renju: from Edo-period gomoku to the name renju, the international federation and the computer proofs"]},{"h":"Five in a row around the world","p":["In the 19th century the game reached Britain as “Go Bang”. Today similar games are played all over the world: Korea’s omok, Vietnam’s caro, and pente, in which stones can be captured in pairs.","This Atlas’s gomoku is played on a 15 × 15 board with a choice of three rules: renju, standard and freestyle. Try them side by side and see how much the forbidden moves of renju tame the first player."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["mid-18th c.","Gomoku is said to be played in Edo Japan"],["1856","The oldest surviving gomoku book, the Gomoku Jōseki Collection"],["1899","Ruikō Kuroiwa names the game renju"],["1988","The Renju International Federation is founded in Stockholm"],["1994","Allis proves freestyle gomoku a first-player win"],["2001","Wágner and Virág show renju without opening rules is a first-player win"]],"figL":{"time":[["1700s","Japan (Edo)","Gomoku is played"],["1899","Japan","Named “renju”"],["1988","Stockholm","The RIF is founded"],["1994","Netherlands","Gomoku: first player wins"],["2001","Hungary","Renju solved for Black"]]}},"carrom":{"title":"Carrom: flick it into the pocket","lead":"Carrom is played across India and South Asia by families and neighbours. Pieces flicked with a finger are sent into pockets at the four corners of the board — a game rather like billiards.","s":["Carrom is played on a smooth board about 74 centimetres square. There is a pocket in each corner; two players sit facing each other, or four play in pairs.","There are 19 pieces: 9 white, 9 black and a red “queen”. On your turn you place the larger, heavier “striker” on the line in front of you — the baseline — and flick it with a finger at the pieces.","You win by pocketing all the pieces of your colour. But the red queen only becomes yours if, after pocketing it, you go on to pocket one of your own pieces to “cover” it.","Carrom is said to come from India; a glass-topped board is still on show in a palace in Patiala, in northern India. State tournaments were held in India in the early 20th century.","In 1988 the International Carrom Federation was founded in Chennai, India, and world championships have been held since 1991. Similar flicking games are found around the world, from Canada’s crokinole to Latvia’s novuss."],"quiz":[["Not counting the striker, how many pieces does carrom have?","19","16","24","32"],["How does the red queen become yours?","Pocket one of your own pieces next (cover it)","Pocket it last","Pocket it on the first shot","Hit it twice in a row"],["In which city was the International Carrom Federation founded in 1988?","Chennai","London","Colombo","Mumbai"]],"kf":["A board with a pocket in each corner: flick the striker with a finger to sink the pieces","9 white, 9 black and a red queen; the queen must be “covered” by sinking one of your own next","Said to come from India; International Carrom Federation 1988, world championships since 1991"],"more":[{"h":"The board and its 19 pieces","p":["Each corner of the board has a netted pocket, and a double baseline is drawn along each side. The striker is placed so that it touches both lines, and flicked from there.","At the start, the queen goes in the middle of the board and the white and black pieces are set round it in a flower. The first shot, which scatters the flower, is the “break” that begins the game."],"cap":["The carrom board and the opening position. The red queen in the middle, the white and black pieces round it like a flower. Pockets in the corners, a double baseline on each side; the striker (blue ring) is placed on the near baseline"]},{"h":"Flick and rebound","p":["The striker is usually flicked with a fingernail; some players use the middle finger, the index finger or the thumb. Sink one of your pieces and you shoot again; miss and it is your opponent’s turn.","The cushions are lively, so players use rebounds as in billiards. Sinking the striker itself is a foul and is penalised."],"cap":["The striker is flicked (blue), rebounds off the right cushion and hits a white piece, which runs into the top-right pocket (red). A piece you cannot aim at directly can still be reached off the cushion"]},{"h":"A family of flicking games","p":["Flicking games like carrom are found all over the world. Canada’s crokinole aims at a hole in the middle of a round board, and pichenotte has long been played in Canada too. Carrom itself is said to have reached North America in the late 19th century with missionaries returning from Asia.","Novuss from Latvia and Estonia is said to have appeared in the 1920s; it is played with a small cue instead of a finger. It became one of the most popular sports in Latvia, and international championships have been held since 1993."],"cap":["Cousins of carrom played by flicking with a finger or a cue: crokinole and pichenotte from Canada, novuss from Latvia and Estonia, and couronne from Scandinavia"]},{"h":"Points and world championships","p":["Under international rules, covering the queen adds 3 points, and the first player to reach 25 points wins. Families play many local variations, such as making the queen worth 5.","Serious tournaments are said to have begun in Sri Lanka around 1935, and by 1958 India and Sri Lanka had official federations. The International Carrom Federation (ICF) was founded in Chennai in 1988, and world championships have been held since 1991."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["early 20th c.","State tournaments are held in India"],["c. 1935","Serious tournaments are said to begin in Sri Lanka"],["1958","India and Sri Lanka have official federations"],["1988","The International Carrom Federation is founded in Chennai"],["1991","The first World Carrom Championship"]],"figL":{"family":[["Carrom","India · South Asia"],["Crokinole","Canada"],["Pichenotte","Canada"],["Novuss","Latvia · Estonia"],["Couronne","Sweden · Denmark"]]}},"crokinole":{"title":"Aiming for the hole in the middle","lead":"Crokinole has long been loved in rural Canada. Discs are flicked with a finger across a round wooden board, aiming for the small hole in the middle. The oldest known board dates from 1876.","s":["Crokinole is played on a round wooden board about 66 centimetres across. Two players sit facing each other, or four play in pairs, flicking their discs with a finger towards the centre.","The board is marked with circles: from the outside, rings worth 5, 10 and 15 points, and in the middle a small hole worth 20. Eight wooden pegs stand round the 15 ring, blocking the straight path in.","Simply aiming at the centre is not enough. When an opponent’s disc is on the board, your shot must hit one; miss, and your own disc is taken off.","The oldest known board was made in 1876 in Ontario, Canada, by the craftsman Eckhardt Wettlaufer, reportedly as a fifth-birthday gift for his son. It is now in a museum in Kitchener.","The name is thought to come from the French croquignole, “a flick”. Since 1999 a world championship has been held every June in Tavistock, where Wettlaufer lived."],"quiz":[["How many points is the hole in the middle of a crokinole board worth?","20","15","10","50"],["When an opponent’s disc is on the board, what must your shot do?","Hit an opponent’s disc","Go into the centre hole","Leave the board","Hit a peg"],["When and where was the oldest known crokinole board made?","Canada, 1876","France, 1776","The United States, 1926","England, 1676"]],"kf":["A round wooden board: flick discs at the 5, 10 and 15 rings and the 20-point hole in the middle","If an opponent’s disc is on the board you must hit it; miss, and your disc is removed","The oldest board: Ontario, Canada, 1876; a world championship since 1999"],"more":[{"h":"Rings, pegs and the ditch","p":["Discs are shot from the outermost 5 ring, within your own quarter. Outside the rim runs a ditch, where discs that fall off or are removed end up.","A disc that drops into the 20 hole is taken out at once and counted as 20. The discs left on the board at the end score 5, 10 or 15 by the ring they lie in, and usually only the difference between the two sides is scored."],"cap":["The crokinole board: rings worth 5, 10 and 15 from the outside, and the 20 hole in the middle. Eight pegs guard the 15 ring, and a ditch round the edge catches discs that are removed"]},{"h":"Hit, or be removed","p":["If even one opponent’s disc is on the board, your shot must hit an opponent’s disc, directly or by way of another disc. Miss, and the disc you shot — and any of your discs moved by the shot — are removed.","When no opponent’s disc is on the board, many rule sets require the shot disc to stop inside or touching the 15 ring. So no one can simply hide, and the game becomes a duel of attack and defence."],"cap":["The shot rule. Left: with an opponent’s (black) disc on the board, hitting it with your (red) disc is fine (✓). Right: miss it, and your disc is taken off the board (×)"]},{"h":"From an Ontario board to a world championship","p":["The first Canadian record is said to go back to 1867, and in the United States Joshua K. Ingalls patented a similar game in 1880. Links to flicking games from Britain and South Asia, such as carrom, have also been suggested.","It is sometimes said to be a game of the Mennonites or the Amish, but that is described as a misconception without support. Still, Russian Mennonites are said to have long enjoyed it under the name Knipsbrat, “flick-board”."],"cap":["Crokinole through time: from the first Canadian record to Wettlaufer’s board, an American patent and the world championship at Tavistock"]},{"h":"Championships and play","p":["The world championship has been held since 1999 on the first Saturday of June in Tavistock. An international association was founded in 2009, and there are clubs across North America and Europe.","This Atlas’s crokinole is played in 3D only, with a choice of board looks. Try it for the feel of flicking, as in carrom, and the fun of aiming for rings, as in darts."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1867","The first Canadian record is said to date from this year"],["1876","Wettlaufer makes a board for his son"],["1880","In the US, Ingalls patents a similar game"],["1999","The world championship begins in Tavistock"],["2009","An international association is founded"]],"figL":{"time":[["1867","Canada","First record"],["1876","Ontario","The oldest board"],["1880","United States","Ingalls’s patent"],["1999","Tavistock","World championship begins"],["2009","International","An international body"]]}},"ugolki":{"title":"Hop, hop, and into the far corner","lead":"Russia’s ugolki, America’s Halma and the star-shaped board of “Chinese checkers” are one family: nothing is captured, you hop over pieces until both armies have swapped corners.","s":["Ugolki means “corners” in Russian. White and black set out their pieces in a block in opposite corners of an 8×8 board, and the first to bring every piece into the other’s corner wins.","A move is either one step to a neighbouring empty square, or a hop over a neighbouring piece to the empty square just beyond. You may hop over your own pieces or your opponent’s, and keep hopping as long as you can. Nothing is captured: hopped pieces stay on the board.","The best-known game of this kind is the American Halma. It is said to have been devised around 1883–84 by the surgeon George Howard Monks, from a few ideas of an English game called Hoppity. The name comes from the Greek for “leap”.","In 1892 a star-board version, “Stern-Halma” (Star Halma), appeared in Germany. In 1928 the American firm of Pressman sold it as “Chinese checkers”, and it became a craze — but the game has nothing to do with China.","Nobody knows for sure when ugolki began. One account says it was invented in Europe at the end of the 18th century, but no firm record has been found. Today it is loved by children and adults alike in Russia and the former Soviet lands."],"quiz":[["In ugolki, what happens to a piece that is hopped over?","It stays on the board","It is taken off","It changes colour","It goes back to its own corner"],["When was the game first sold as “Chinese checkers”?","1928, in the USA","1892, in China","the 1700s, in Qing China","the 1950s, in Japan"],["Who is said to have invented Halma?","Monks, an American surgeon","a German toy company","a Russian mathematician","a Chinese emperor"]],"kf":["From corner to corner on an 8×8 board: bring all your pieces home first. Nothing is captured","Step once, or hop over a neighbouring piece — yours or theirs — and keep hopping","Halma (USA, c. 1883–84) → Stern-Halma (Germany, 1892) → “Chinese checkers” (1928). Not Chinese at all"],"more":[{"h":"Hopping ahead","p":["When you hop, it makes no difference whose piece you hop over. Line your own pieces up as a “ladder”, use the enemy’s as stepping stones too, and one move can cross half the board.","Hopping is never compulsory, and you may stop part-way. But you cannot mix a step and a hop in the same move."],"cap":["A chain of hops: the white piece hops over a black, a white, a black and a white piece in turn, four hops in one move (1–4). Every hopped piece stays on the board"]},{"h":"The ugolki board and camps","p":["Camps come in several sizes: 3×3 (9 pieces), 3×4 (12) and 4×4 (16). In the classic rules pieces move only straight, along rows and columns; a variant also allows diagonal moves.","If you simply sit in your own camp, your opponent can never fill it. So a rule widely used in Russia says that whoever still has a piece in their own camp after move 40 loses. If nothing is decided by move 80, the number of pieces in the enemy camp decides."],"cap":["The ugolki board: white starts in the 3×4 camp at the bottom left (a1–d3), black at the top right (e6–h8), and each heads for the other’s corner (arrows). Camps can also be 3×3 or 4×4"]},{"h":"The Halma family","p":["Halma is played on a 16×16 board, with 19 pieces each for two players or 13 each for four, moving in eight directions. Monks received an American patent in 1888. The mathematician Thomas Hill, later president of Harvard, is said to have given it its name.","The star board has 121 holes; usually each player has 10 pieces, and two, three, four or six can play. In China it is called tiaoqi, “jump chess”, and in Japan a similar game is known as the “diamond game”. Here too, an opponent who stays in the corner you are aiming for can stop you filling it."],"cap":["The family of hop-and-swap games: Halma on a 16×16 board, Stern-Halma and “Chinese checkers” on the star board, ugolki on an 8×8 board"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["The rules of ugolki take a minute to learn. But building long ladders out of the enemy’s pieces too, while leaving no stepping stones for them, makes for a deep battle of reading ahead.","In this Atlas you can play ugolki with 3×3, 3×4 or 4×4 camps, or with diagonal moves. The 3D board is white and cobalt porcelain, like Russian Gzhel ware."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1883","Halma is born (c. 1883–84, USA)"],["1888","Monks receives a US patent for Halma"],["1892","Stern-Halma on a star board, in Germany"],["1928","Sold as “Chinese checkers” in the USA"],["1941","Milton Bradley obtains the rights to the name"]],"figL":{"fam":[["c. 1883–84","Halma","USA · 16×16 board"],["1892","Stern-Halma","Germany · star board"],["1928","Chinese checkers","a new name in the USA"],["date unknown","Ugolki","Russia · 8×8 board"]]}},"agon":{"title":"The queen to the throne, the guards around her","lead":"Agon was played in Victorian England on a board of hexagonal cells. Seat your queen on the throne in the middle and surround her with your six guards, and you win.","s":["Agon is a game for two, also called Queen’s Guards. The name is Greek for “contest”. The board is made of 91 hexagonal cells, and the single cell in the middle is the throne.","Five rings surround the throne, each a different colour from the next. Each player has a queen and six guards; you win by seating your queen on the throne and filling the six cells around it with your guards.","A piece moves one cell at a time, either along the same ring or into the next ring inwards. It can never move outwards, so once it has stepped in, there is no going back. Only a queen may enter the throne.","The oldest known record is a set of rules published in London in 1842. The game is credited to Anthony Peacock of London, and it was enjoyed in Victorian homes.","It is often called the oldest known board game played on hexagonal cells. One story says it was born in 18th-century France, but the old boards cited for this are thought to be later additions, so it remains uncertain."],"quiz":[["How do you win at Agon?","Queen on the throne, ringed by your six guards","Capture the enemy queen","Line all your pieces up round the edge","Surround the empty throne"],["Which way can an Agon piece never move?","Into an outer ring","Along the same ring","Into the next ring inwards","Onto the throne (if it is a queen)"],["When and where were the rules of Agon first published?","London, 1842","Paris, 1742","Berlin, 1892","New York, 1942"]],"kf":["91 hexagonal cells: the throne in the middle, five rings around it","Step along the same ring or inwards, never outwards. Only a queen may take the throne","Published in London in 1842; often called the oldest hex-board game"],"more":[{"h":"A board of throne and rings","p":["In the standard start, both players’ queens and guards alternate round the outermost ring. In the 1842 rules, the queens began in opposite corners and the guards were placed one at a time, in turn.","The board looks like a target, coloured rings round the throne. You can see at a glance which ring a piece is on, which makes it easy to count how many steps it still needs to reach the throne."],"cap":["The Agon board and the starting layout. The green cell in the middle is the throne, ringed by five red and ivory rings in turn. Each side’s queen (crown) and six guards alternate round the edge"]},{"h":"Inwards, ever inwards","p":["Each turn you move one piece one step to a neighbouring empty cell, either around the same ring or inwards.","You may not step into a cell between two enemy pieces. So a wall of enemy pieces is a real barrier on the road inwards."],"cap":["How pieces move. The white guard may step along its ring or into the inner ring (green), but not back out (red ×)"]},{"h":"Sandwiched, and sent out","p":["Trap an enemy piece in a straight line between two of yours and it is captured. Its owner must spend the next turn putting the captured guard back on an empty cell of the outer ring.","A captured queen goes back to any empty cell except the throne, and she is moved first. All the hard-won steps inwards are lost, so defence matters a great deal. Different books give slightly different details of the rules."],"cap":["Capture. (1) The white guard steps in and traps the black guard in a line between two white pieces. (2) On its owner’s next turn, the trapped black guard is moved out to an empty cell on the edge"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["If your six guards surround the throne while it is empty, you lose on the spot. Send the queen in first, or build a road with the guards? Planning the order of moves decides the game.","In this Atlas you can play Agon with the standard start round the edge, or with the 1842 start where the guards are placed one by one. The 3D board is Victorian mahogany with gold lines, with bone and ebony pieces."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["late 1700s","A story of a French origin (no firm evidence)"],["1842","Rules of “Agon, or the Queen’s Guards” published in London"],["1800s","Popular in Victorian England; boards sold by Jaques and others"],["1999","Described in Parlett’s Oxford History of Board Games"],["today","Included in Ludii, a system for studying games"]]},"dots":{"title":"Dots, lines and falling discs — games with an answer","lead":"Dots and Boxes, played with pencil and paper, and Connect Four, played by dropping discs: two games a child can play, which mathematicians and computers set out to solve in earnest.","s":["Dots and Boxes is played on paper with a grid of dots. Two players take turns joining two neighbouring dots with a line; whoever draws the fourth side of a square takes that box and draws another line. Most boxes wins.","The game was introduced as “La Pipopipette” by the French mathematician Édouard Lucas, in 1889 by most accounts. After his death it also appeared in his book L’Arithmétique amusante (1895).","Connect Four is played by dropping discs into an upright frame of 7 columns and 6 rows. A disc falls to the lowest empty hole, and four of your colour in a row — across, up or diagonally — wins. The American firm Milton Bradley began selling it in 1974.","In October 1988 Connect Four was “solved”. On the 1st James Dow Allen, and on the 16th Victor Allis, each working alone, showed that the first player can always win by starting in the middle column.","For Dots and Boxes, too, the mathematician Elwyn Berlekamp and others built up a theory of “long chains”, gathered in a book in 2000. Behind a game anyone can play lay deep mathematics."],"quiz":[["In Dots and Boxes, what does the player who draws a box’s fourth side do?","Takes the box and draws another line","Passes the turn","Gives the box away","Ends the game"],["In Connect Four, which first move wins for the first player?","The middle column","The leftmost column","The rightmost column","Any column"],["Who independently solved Connect Four in 1988?","Allen and Allis","Lucas and Berlekamp","Wexler and Strongin","Conway and Guy"]],"kf":["Dots and Boxes: close a box, take it, draw again. Whoever opens the first long chain usually loses","Connect Four: 7 columns × 6 rows, discs fall to the bottom. On sale from 1974","Solved by Allen and Allis separately in 1988: the first player wins from the centre"],"more":[{"h":"Long chains and the double-cross","p":["Open a “chain” of connected boxes and your opponent can take them all. So the endgame becomes a fight over who will be forced to open the long chains first. Whoever opens the first long chain usually loses.","The player taking a chain can deliberately leave the last two boxes for the opponent (the double-cross). Two boxes lost, but the opponent must open the next chain, and the big chains that remain are yours."],"cap":["The battle of the long chain. (1) B opens a chain of five boxes. A takes three, then instead of the last two draws the end line (2) and hands them over. (3) B takes both with one line but must then draw again — and open the next chain"]},{"h":"Falling discs, odd rows and even rows","p":["You cannot place a disc wherever you like: the columns fill from the bottom. So even a winning hole cannot be used while the hole beneath it is empty — and whoever fills that hole first hands the win to the other side.","As the frame fills, the number of such “holes nobody wants to fill” decides the game. In Allis’s analysis, threats on odd rows are said to be strong for the first player, and threats on even rows for the second."],"cap":["The Connect Four frame. A disc dropped in at the top falls to the lowest empty hole. Red has a threat on the third row, an odd row (red dashes). Whoever fills the hole beneath it (×) hands red the win"]},{"h":"A timeline of solved games","p":["Connect Four is credited to the Americans Howard Wexler and Ned Strongin. A similar game is also called “the Captain’s Mistress”, from a story that Captain Cook spent hours playing it in his cabin — but no firm evidence for the story has been found.","According to Allen’s and Allis’s analyses, the first player starting in the middle wins by the 41st move at the latest; starting in a column next to it gives a draw, and starting in any of the outer four lets the second player win. The theory of Dots and Boxes was set out at length in Winning Ways (1982)."],"cap":["Steps on the way to solving games: Lucas’s pipopipette, Connect Four going on sale, the year Connect Four was solved, Berlekamp’s book"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["Knowing the answer is one thing; playing against a person is quite another. Counting chains, counting odd and even rows, staying one step ahead of your opponent — the fun is the same.","This Atlas has both Dots and Boxes and Connect Four. Draw lines on a notebook or a slate, drop discs into a leather or ice frame, and catch a glimpse of what the mathematicians saw."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1889","Lucas introduces “La Pipopipette” (by most accounts)"],["1974","Milton Bradley puts Connect Four on sale"],["1982","Winning Ways analyses Dots and Boxes"],["1988","Allen and Allis solve Connect Four separately"],["2000","Berlekamp’s The Dots and Boxes Game"]],"figL":{"time":[["1889","La Pipopipette","Lucas · France"],["1974","Connect Four","Milton Bradley"],["1988","Connect Four solved","Allen and Allis · first player wins"],["2000","The Dots and Boxes Game","Berlekamp’s book"]]}},"hitblow":{"title":"Find the hidden four — the family of Hit & Blow","lead":"Guess four hidden colours or digits with nothing but “hits” and “blows” for clues. The game grew from a pencil-and-paper pastime into the coloured pegs of Mastermind, and on into computer science.","s":["In Hit & Blow, one player secretly sets out four colours or digits. When the other makes a guess, the only reply is how many are right in colour and place (hits) and how many are the right colour in the wrong place (blows).","It grew from a pencil-and-paper number game, Bulls and Cows: hits are bulls and blows are cows. It is sometimes said to be a century old, but its real origin is not known.","In 1970 Mordecai Meirowitz, an Israeli postmaster and telecommunications expert, turned it into a board game with coloured pegs. Invicta Plastics, near Leicester in England, bought the rights and launched it as Mastermind in 1971–72, and it went round the world.","At about the same time, computers were playing the number game too. Before 1970 a version called MOO was written for the Titan computer at Cambridge University, and in 1972 it was ported to Multics at MIT in the United States.","In 1976–77 the computer scientist Donald Knuth showed that in Mastermind with 6 colours and 4 holes (1,296 codes in all), the right guesses always find the code in at most five tries."],"quiz":[["When a guessed colour is in the same place as in the code, what is it called?","A hit","A blow","A miss","A pair"],["Who invented Mastermind with its coloured pegs?","Meirowitz, in Israel","Knuth, in the USA","the British Post Office","students at Cambridge University"],["How many guesses did Knuth show are always enough for Mastermind with 6 colours and 4 holes?","At most five","At most three","At most eight","At most ten"]],"kf":["A hit is right colour, right place; a blow is right colour, wrong place. You are never told which ones","Pencil-and-paper Bulls and Cows → Meirowitz’s coloured pegs in 1970 → Mastermind in 1971–72","Knuth (1976–77): all 1,296 codes of 6 colours × 4 holes can be found in at most five guesses"],"more":[{"h":"Hits and blows","p":["The pins never tell you which places were hits — only that there was “one hit and two blows”. So you combine the answers from guess to guess, narrowing down what the code can be.","Repeating a guess tells you nothing. The usual way is to choose an arrangement that fits every answer so far, but sometimes a guess that cannot be right gathers more information and is the shorter road."],"cap":["One guess and its answer. Above, the hidden code; below, the guess. The leftmost is the right colour in the right place: a hit (pink pin). The second and fourth are colours in the code but in the wrong place: blows (white pins). The third is nowhere in it (×)"]},{"h":"Every answer shrinks the field","p":["Knuth’s method picks, each time, the guess that leaves the fewest codes even if the worst possible answer comes back. Its first move uses two colours twice each.","Later work found the method with the lowest average, too: in 1993 Koyama and Lai showed about 4.34 guesses on average (with six in the worst case). The number game Bulls and Cows, with four different digits, can always be solved within seven guesses."],"cap":["One code found with Knuth’s method. The 1,296 codes of 6 colours × 4 holes drop to 256 after the first guess, 41 after the second, 6 after the third and 1 after the fourth; the fifth guess is right (bars on a log scale)"]},{"h":"From numbers to colours","p":["Mastermind gained many relatives, among them Super Mastermind with 8 colours and 5 holes (1972) and Word Mastermind, where you guess letters.","In Japan the number-guessing game is known as “Hit & Blow”, and it has appeared on television and in collections of games for consoles."],"cap":["The family in time: pencil-and-paper Bulls and Cows, MOO on the Cambridge computer, Meirowitz’s Mastermind and Knuth’s “five”"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["The best moment is when, comparing the clue rows, you realise “this colour can only go here”. If every guess cuts the field by half or more, you are playing like a master.","Hit & Blow in this Atlas has five rule sets — 4 of 6 colours with no repeats (360 codes), with repeats (Knuth’s 1,296), 3 or 4 digits, and an expert game of 5 from 8 colours — plus a puzzle of the day."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1970","MOO on the Cambridge computer; Meirowitz devises the coloured-peg board"],["1971","Invicta Plastics buys the rights and brings out Mastermind"],["1972","MOO reaches Multics at MIT; Super Mastermind"],["1977","Knuth: “at most five” (paper of 1976–77)"],["1993","Koyama and Lai: a method averaging about 4.34"]],"figL":{"time":[["?","Bulls and Cows","pencil-and-paper numbers"],["1970","MOO","the Cambridge computer"],["1971","Mastermind","Meirowitz · Invicta"],["1977","Always in five","Knuth’s proof"]]}},"botao":{"title":"Football with the buttons off your coat","lead":"Brazil’s futebol de botão is table football played by flicking buttons to send a tiny ball into the goal. A pocket game of children became a sport recognised by the state.","s":["Futebol de botão means “button football”. On a field about 167 cm long, each team sets out ten round buttons and a rectangular block for a goalkeeper, and flicks the buttons to drive a felt ball about 1 cm across into the other goal.","It is said to have begun early in the 20th century, when children in Brazil and Hungary pulled the buttons off their coats and shirts to play. In Hungary it is still called gombfoci, “button football”.","In Brazil the game was put in order by Geraldo Décourt, an artist from Rio de Janeiro. In 1930 (1929 in some sources) he published the first rule book, naming the game “Foot-Ball Celotex” after the board covering it was played on.","A São Paulo state federation followed in 1962, and in 1988 Brazil’s National Sports Council recognised “futebol de mesa” (table football) as an official sport. Décourt’s birthday, 14 February, is Button Players’ Day in São Paulo state.","Other countries have flicking football too. In Britain a game of the same kind was born in Liverpool in 1929, and a table football played by flicking little figures appeared in 1947; table football, with figures turned on rods, was being patented from the 1920s."],"quiz":[["What makes up a team in futebol de botão?","Ten buttons and a goalkeeper","Eleven buttons","Five buttons and a goalkeeper","Eleven figures"],["When was it recognised as an official sport in Brazil?","1988","1930","1962","2001"],["In the table football born in Britain in 1947, what do you flick?","Weighted player figures","Coat buttons","Bottle caps","Coins"]],"kf":["Table football with ten buttons and a keeper’s block, flicked to drive a felt ball","A children’s game early in the 1900s → Décourt’s rule book in 1930 → an official sport in Brazil in 1988","Cousins: Hungary’s gombfoci, Britain’s flicking table football (1929 and 1947), and rod-and-figure table football"],"more":[{"h":"Flick, declare, shoot","p":["You can flick a button with a finger, but many players press its edge with a flat tool called a palheta, so that it slides forward. The button must strike the ball itself.","Under the 12-touch rules you call out your shot first and wait while the defender sets the keeper; only then may you shoot. A goal without a declaration does not count."],"cap":["How a button is flicked. (1) Press the button’s edge with a flat tool and it slides into the ball. (2) The ball passes the keeper (the blue block) and goes into the goal"]},{"h":"Rules that change with the touches","p":["Brazil has traditional rules from different regions: the Bahia style, one touch with a disc; the Rio style, three touches with a ball; and the São Paulo style, twelve touches with a ball.","With twelve touches you may flick up to twelve times in one possession, and the same button up to three times in a row. The fewer the touches, the fewer the goals; the one-touch game, where turns change at every flick, is said to be for experts."],"cap":["A team is ten buttons and a rectangular keeper. Brazil’s three classic rule sets differ in how many flicks (touches) one possession allows"]},{"h":"The flicking-football family","p":["The British table football of 1947, played by flicking figures with a finger, is said to have been modelled on a game of the same kind born in Liverpool in 1929. You flick small weighted player figures to move the ball.","Table football, where you turn rows of figures on rods to kick the ball, was patented in Britain in 1921, and in Spain Alejandro Finisterre patented his futbolín in 1937."],"cap":["The family of flicking table football: Hungary’s gombfoci, two British flicking games, and table football with figures on rods"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["Pass the ball upfield, call your shot, aim at the gap beside the keeper: the strength and angle of a single press decide the match.","Futebol de botão in this Atlas is a 3D-only game based on the 12-touch rules. You can choose matches by moves, by goals or a penalty shoot-out, and fields of grass, wood and more."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["early 1900s","Children in Hungary and Brazil play with buttons"],["1930","Décourt’s first rule book (1929 in some sources)"],["1947","A table football of flicked figures is born in Britain"],["1962","A São Paulo state federation is founded"],["1988","Recognised as an official sport in Brazil"]],"figL":{"rules":[["1","Bahia style\ndisc"],["3","Rio style\nball"],["12","São Paulo\nball"]],"family":[["Futebol de botão","Brazil · a rule book of 1930"],["Gombfoci","Hungary · coat buttons"],["A Liverpool game","Britain · 1929 · the same kind"],["Figure table football","Britain · 1947 · flicked figures"],["Table football","figures on rods · patent of 1921"]]}},"hockey":{"title":"From a kitchen board to an air rink","lead":"Table hockey comes in two kinds: rod hockey, where rods move the players, and air hockey, where a puck floating on air is struck back and forth. This is the story of a homemade Canadian board and a team of American engineers.","s":["Table hockey is hockey on a small rink on a tabletop. Broadly there are two kinds: rod hockey, where rods under the board move little player figures, and air hockey, where the puck floats on a cushion of air and is struck with mallets.","The first table hockey game is said to have been made in Toronto, Canada, in 1932 by Donald Munro. With too little money for Christmas in the Depression, he built a board for his children from scrap wood, wire and clock springs. It went on sale in a Toronto department store and caught on.","In rod hockey the figures stand in slots cut through the board and are linked to rods underneath. Push or pull a rod and the player slides along its slot; spin it and the player swings the stick. The game later flourished in the Nordic countries and elsewhere, and has an international federation and world championships.","In 1969 engineers at the American company Brunswick began work on a game played on a low-friction surface. Bob Lemieux revived the stalled project, and “air hockey”, played with a thin disc, two mallets and slit-like goals, was born; it went on sale in the early 1970s.","Floating things on a table of air was an older idea, used for moving heavy loads and in physics classroom equipment. Air hockey was a hit: by the mid-1970s tournaments were held in the United States, and a world championship began in 1978."],"quiz":[["Where is the first table hockey game said to have been made?","Toronto, 1932","Chicago, 1972","Stockholm, 1900","Tokyo, 1950"],["Why does an air hockey puck keep sliding?","It floats on air from tiny holes in the table","It is made of ice","The table slopes","It has wheels underneath"],["How do you move a player in rod hockey?","Push, pull and spin a rod under the board","Flick the player with a finger","Pull it with a magnet","Pull it on a string"]],"kf":["Rod hockey: push, pull and spin rods under the board to move players in their slots","Air hockey: air from holes in the table floats the puck, which is struck with mallets","A homemade board in Toronto, 1932 → research from 1969 → air hockey in the early 1970s"],"more":[{"h":"Players in slots","p":["With several players to handle on your own, switching quickly between rods is the whole game. The basic plan is to pass and aim at the side away from the goalie.","Munro’s first board is said to have peaked in the middle and sloped to each end, with a lever for the goalie and flippers for the others. The spinning rods of today spread later."],"cap":["A rod hockey board. Each player stands in a slot and is moved by a rod under the board: (1) push or pull the rod to slide along the slot; (2) spin it to swing the stick (the dashed line is the rod under the board)"]},{"h":"A puck floating on air","p":["With almost no friction the puck rebounds from the rails again and again. The bank shot, changing the angle off a rail, is a basic skill; defenders dart to the corners of their goal, making a triangle.","Tournament tables are 8 feet (about 2.4 m) long. The striker is called a mallet, paddle or striker, and the puck is made of hard plastic."],"cap":["An air hockey table. Air blows up through countless tiny holes (arrows, left), and the puck floats a hair above the surface and slides on and on. Struck by the red mallet, it banks off the right rail and goes past the blue mallet guarding the middle of the goal (dashes)"]},{"h":"Table hockey in time","p":["In the 1980s rod hockey tables under a clear dome became popular in arcades. Table football, with figures on rods kicking a ball, belongs to the same “moved by rods” family.","In Japan, too, air hockey became an arcade favourite from around the 1980s, and small tables are made for the home."],"cap":["Table hockey in time: rod hockey in Toronto, 1932; research on air tables from 1969; air hockey in the early 1970s; a world championship from 1978"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["Defend, use the rails, slip past the gap beside the other mallet: eyes that read a fast puck and quick hands decide the game.","Table hockey in this Atlas is 3D-only, with six tables including a small felt rink and an air table. Use the edge shot — a glancing hit off the rim of your pad — to beat the other goal."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1932","Munro builds a table hockey board in Toronto"],["1969","Research on a game on an air table begins in the USA"],["1972","Air hockey goes on sale (early 1970s)"],["1975","An air hockey association is founded in the USA"],["1978","An air hockey world championship begins"]],"figL":{"time":[["1932","Rod hockey","Toronto · made at home"],["1969","Air table research","American engineers"],["1972","Air hockey","put on sale by Brunswick"],["1978","World championship","air hockey tournaments"]]}},"solitaire":{"title":"Patience: cards laid out for one","lead":"Solitaire, the card game you play alone, is called “patience” in Britain. From an 18th-century German book to the computer screen, this is the story of cards played on your own.","s":["Solitaire is the name for card games played by one person. You deal the cards into a set layout, move them by the rules, and win if you build every suit up from Ace to King. In Britain the games are called “patience”; “solitaire” spread in North America in the 20th century.","The earliest known description is in the 1783 edition of a German games book, under the name “Patience”. The games probably began in Germany or Scandinavia, and a link has been suggested with the fortune-telling layouts that spread a little earlier. In Danish and Norwegian they are called kabal.","Patience became popular in France early in the 19th century and reached Britain and America later in that century. A book of patiences alone appeared in Russia in 1826. In English, Lady Adelaide Cadogan’s Illustrated Games of Patience, from around 1870, is well known.","The most famous game, Klondike, is played with seven piles. The earliest record of its rules is in a book of 1907. Some say the name comes from the Canadian gold rush, but there is no firm evidence.","In 1990 Klondike came bundled with personal computers, and people all over the world began to play. FreeCell, with every card dealt face up, was created in 1978 by Paul Alfille, a medical student, on a university computer."],"quiz":[["What is solitaire called in Britain?","Patience","Kabal","Klondike","FreeCell"],["Where is the earliest record of solitaire?","A German book, 1783 edition","A French book of about 1650","An American book of 1907","A computer of 1990"],["Among the 32,000 numbered deals of the FreeCell bundled with computers, how many cannot be solved?","Only one, #11982","Exactly half","One in every hundred","None at all"]],"kf":["Rearrange cards alone and build each suit from Ace to King to win. In Britain it is “patience”","Earliest record: a German book, 1783 edition. Probably born in Germany or Scandinavia","Klondike’s earliest rules are in a book of 1907; from 1990 computers spread it worldwide"],"more":[{"h":"The Klondike layout","p":["Twenty-eight cards go into the seven piles and the other 24 form the stock. On the piles you build downward in alternating red and black. Only a King may go into an empty pile.","How often Klondike can be won with good play is still not known. Even if every face-down card were known, a little over eight in ten games is calculated to be the most that can be won."],"cap":["The Klondike layout. Top left, the stock and the turned-up waste; top right, the four foundations built by suit. Below, seven piles of one to seven cards, only the top card face up"]},{"h":"FreeCell, almost always solvable","p":["In FreeCell you rearrange cards that are all face up, using four free cells that can each hold one card. It is a game of reading ahead rather than luck, and nearly every deal can be solved.","Of deals 1 to 32,000 in the game bundled with computers, only #11982 could not be solved. A 2018 study of 8.6 billion deals found about one unsolvable deal in 84,000."],"cap":["The FreeCell layout. Top left, four free cells; top right, four foundations; below, eight piles all face up. The strip below covers deals 1 to 32,000 of the game bundled with computers: only #11982 cannot be solved"]},{"h":"Patience in time","p":["FreeCell grew from Baker’s Game, which Martin Gardner described in a magazine in 1968. At the end of the 19th century Mary Whitmore Jones of England collected some 250 patiences in her books.","Elsewhere, “solitaire” can also mean one-player board games such as peg solitaire. The games historian David Parlett prefers “patience”, which means only the card games."],"cap":["Patience in time: a German book of 1783, an English book of about 1870, the Klondike rules of 1907, FreeCell in 1978 and computers in 1990"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["Solitaire is a game for quiet moments that tidies the mind. Which pile to break up first, which card to keep back? The further ahead you think, the more often you win.","Solitaire in this Atlas offers Klondike, Spider and FreeCell, and a deal of the day. True to the name patience, lay them out slowly."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1783","“Patience” appears in a German book (earliest record)"],["1826","A book of patiences alone appears in Russia"],["c. 1870","Lady Adelaide Cadogan’s Illustrated Games of Patience"],["1907","The Klondike rules appear in a book"],["1990","Spread worldwide by a game bundled with computers"]],"figL":{"time":[["1783","Patience","earliest record, a German book"],["c. 1870","A book in English","Lady Adelaide Cadogan"],["1907","Klondike","earliest record of its rules"],["1978","FreeCell","born on a university computer"],["1990","On computers","spread by a bundled game"]]}},"sudoku":{"title":"The digits must be single","lead":"Sudoku: put 1 to 9 into a 9×9 grid. Born in an American magazine, named in Japan and spread worldwide from British newspapers, it needs no arithmetic — only logic.","s":["Sudoku has a single rule. Fill the 9×9 grid so that every row, every column and every 3×3 box outlined in bold holds each digit from 1 to 9 exactly once. No adding or subtracting is needed. A good puzzle has exactly one solution.","A finished sudoku is a kind of what mathematicians call a Latin square. The 18th-century mathematician Leonhard Euler is known for studying them, but there is no direct link to the puzzle itself. In 1892 and 1895 French newspapers printed number-filling magic-square puzzles, but they needed arithmetic or had no boxes, so they were not yet the sudoku we know.","The modern puzzle first appeared in 1979 in an American puzzle magazine, under the name “Number Place”. Its maker is thought to have been Howard Garns, a retired architect.","In 1984 a Japanese puzzle magazine (Nikoli) introduced it as “the digits must be single”, and in 1986 the company’s president Maki Kaji shortened that to “sudoku”. In Japan the name is the company’s registered trademark, and other publishers call the puzzle “nanpure” and the like.","Wayne Gould, who came across a sudoku book in Tokyo in 1997, spent years writing a program to make puzzles, and in November 2004 sudoku appeared in the British newspaper The Times. By 2005 it was a craze in newspapers all over the world, and a world championship has been held since 2006."],"quiz":[["In sudoku, what must hold 1 to 9 exactly once?","Every row, every column and every 3×3 box","Only the columns","Only the diagonals","Only the outer edge"],["What is the fewest clues a sudoku with one solution can have?","17","9","25","30"],["Where and when did the modern puzzle first appear in a magazine?","USA, 1979","Japan, 1984","France, 1892","Britain, 2004"]],"kf":["Each row, column and 3×3 box holds 1–9 once. No arithmetic needed","At least 17 clues: an exhaustive search in 2012 showed that no 16-clue puzzle has one solution","“Number Place” in the USA, 1979 → named in Japan, 1984 → worldwide from Britain, 2004"],"more":[{"h":"Rows, columns and boxes","p":["The most basic idea in solving sudoku is to find “this digit can only go here”. Once a digit is placed, no other cell in its row, column or box can hold the same digit.","As in the picture, when 5s elsewhere close off the rows and columns of the top-left box, only one cell remains for its 5. A good puzzle can be solved to the end by such small steps alone, with no guessing."],"cap":["The constraints. We want the 5 for the top-left box (green frame). The four other 5s block its rows and columns (red bands), so only the green cell is left for its 5"]},{"h":"From seventeen clues","p":["Remove clues one by one, and at some point the answer is no longer unique. In 2012 Gary McGuire of Ireland and his colleagues showed, by a long exhaustive computer search, that no 16-clue sudoku has a single solution: the minimum is 17.","It was also calculated, in 2005, that there are about 6.67 × 10²¹ completed sudoku grids in all. More clues do not always mean an easier puzzle: difficulty depends on where the clues are placed."],"cap":["A sudoku with only 17 clues; it still has exactly one solution. No sudoku with 16 clues has a single solution"]},{"h":"Sudoku in time","p":["The name “sudoku” is short for “the digits must be single” — one digit to a cell. In English, too, it is simply called sudoku.","There are many relatives with slightly changed rules: “killer”, where the sum inside each cage is the clue; “jigsaw”, with irregular boxes; and huge puzzles of five overlapping grids."],"cap":["Sudoku in time: Euler’s Latin squares, French newspaper puzzles, America’s “Number Place”, the naming in Japan and the worldwide boom that began in Britain"]},{"h":"Try it","p":["With a pencil, paper and a little insight you can play anywhere. When you are stuck, pick one digit and scan the whole grid for it.","This Atlas has sudoku with new puzzles every day at three levels, and speed sudoku, a race on a small 4×4 grid."],"cap":[]}],"tl":[["1782","Euler studies Latin squares (the mathematical root)"],["1892","A number-filling puzzle in a French newspaper"],["1979","“Number Place” in an American magazine"],["1984","Introduced in Japan; named “sudoku” in 1986"],["2004","A worldwide craze begins in British newspapers"]],"figL":{"time":[["1782","Latin squares","Euler’s study · the maths"],["1892","French papers","number-filling puzzles"],["1979","Number Place","an American magazine"],["1984","“Sudoku”","named in a Japanese magazine"],["2004","Worldwide boom","from British newspapers"]]}}};
